window.__SITE_DATA__={"base":"/","preview":true,"search":[{"id":"AI-01","kind":"station","number":1,"line":"A","line_name":"Strategy & Value","line_color":"#147AD6","title":"Buying AI licences and calling it a strategy","short":"Strategy, not licences","summary":"Define the business change before treating tool access as progress.","text":"Station 01 (AI-01) Line A (Strategy & Value): Strategy, not licences — Buying AI licences and calling it a strategy. Define the business change before treating tool access as progress. A rollout plan is substituting for a business hypothesis when it names licences, training and usage targets but cannot identify the work that should change. The missing decision is not necessarily which technology to buy. It may be who receives the benefit, what constraint matters and who can change the process. Separate exploratory access from a committed business improvement programme. Which business outcome is this initiative meant to change, for whom, and against what baseline? Which workflow, decision or service will work differently—not merely faster within the same step? Who owns the benefit and can change the process, staffing priorities or service design? Write a one-page use-case charter naming the problem, affected users, current baseline, proposed change and accountable business owner. Choose a bounded workflow and state what evidence would justify continuing, changing or stopping; keep a separate budget for open-ended learning. Compare AI with process simplification, existing software and doing nothing before committing to a delivery architecture. Levie’s supplied post distinguishes compute consumption from useful results and says the answer depends on individual workflows. It is a useful challenge to an adoption dashboard: a rising activity line still needs an explanation of which business work improved. The Reshuffle note shifts attention from automating separate tasks to assembling them into a useful service. Reducing one task’s cost may move the bottleneck to integration, trust, demand or accountable delivery. This is a reason to measure the larger outcome as well as the local improvement. The Commoncog note describes effectual reasoning: begin with available means, take an affordable action, learn from it and adjust as partners or constraints become clearer. This supports a pilot designed to answer a decision-relevant question rather than one expected to validate an already fixed plan. Software tools address a global software market of roughly $500 billion, whereas businesses spend over $10 trillion annually on services and labor. Buying copilot licenses treats AI as a tooling line-item that assists existing workers, whereas autopilots sell completed end-work directly. Confusing a tooling purchase with workflow delegation obscures the actual economic shift from software seat-licensing to labor substitution.","url":"/stations/01-licences-are-not-strategy/"},{"id":"AI-02","kind":"station","number":2,"line":"A","line_name":"Strategy & Value","line_color":"#147AD6","title":"Automating a process nobody understands","short":"Understand the work","summary":"Map real work, including its exceptions and informal decisions, before deciding what to automate.","text":"Station 02 (AI-02) Line A (Strategy & Value): Understand the work — Automating a process nobody understands. Map real work, including its exceptions and informal decisions, before deciding what to automate. A workflow diagram can be technically correct and operationally false. Work may actually pass through private spreadsheets, calls, queues and improvised interpretations. Some missing steps are undocumented knowledge; others are unresolved decisions about how the business should operate. Distinguish those two situations so that an engineer is not asked to invent policy while building an integration. Can a person follow one ordinary case and one difficult case from trigger to accepted completion? Where does the work wait, change format, need clarification or rely on an unnamed expert? Which steps are unnecessary, and which apparent inefficiencies protect quality, relationships or accountability? Observe real cases with frontline workers; capture inputs, transformations, handoffs, waits, decisions, exceptions and completion evidence. Assign business owners to unresolved rules before implementation; distinguish historical practice from approved policy. Redesign the smallest useful end-to-end slice, comparing elimination, simplification and automation rather than preserving every existing step. Ajzenstadt’s forecast-variance example requires agreement about forecasts, actuals, thresholds and who receives the result. Some of the apparent implementation work is therefore deciding how the business should operate. An agent cannot reliably recover a policy that the organisation has never agreed. Pierce’s guide starts with an inventory of workflows and asks how work starts, how it moves and what completion means. It then separates tools to retain, absorb or remove. The useful contribution is the sequence of diagnostic questions, rather than the assumption that every business needs one replacement system. Giving each department an AI assistant speeds up local tasks while leaving inter-departmental contradictions intact—such as sales quoting delivery dates that purchasing cannot meet due to supplier delays. Genuine workflow redesign requires comparing real-world execution with the planning assumptions used to make commitments across departmental boundaries.","url":"/stations/02-understand-before-automating/"},{"id":"AI-03","kind":"station","number":3,"line":"A","line_name":"Strategy & Value","line_color":"#147AD6","title":"Choosing projects for spectacle rather than business value","short":"Value over spectacle","summary":"Choose useful problems and make room for bounded discovery—not just impressive demonstrations.","text":"Station 03 (AI-03) Line A (Strategy & Value): Value over spectacle — Choosing projects for spectacle rather than business value. Choose useful problems and make room for bounded discovery—not just impressive demonstrations. A project can be visually impressive yet disconnected from a meaningful constraint. A small task can also be too small to matter. The decision should consider frequency, consequence, feasibility, adoption effort and the ability to turn a local gain into a better service. Separate improvements to current work from experiments that may reveal an entirely new capability. Who will repeatedly use the result, and what work becomes easier or newly possible? Does the proposed change relieve the current constraint or produce more work for another queue? Is this an optimisation project with a known outcome or an exploration whose first deliverable is information? Build a short opportunity portfolio scored against customer value, workflow friction, risk, data access and practical ownership; keep scoring transparent rather than pretend it is precise prediction. Reserve a bounded exploration lane for ideas that do not yet have a stable business case; ask what each experiment taught. Prefer a credible route from a small win to a useful operating change over isolated demos that cannot be maintained or adopted. Pearson’s editorial-workflow example distinguishes lower preparation effort from the ability to reconsider more research and produce more tailored work. Those are different benefits: one releases capacity; the other expands a feasible service. Neither should automatically be translated into cash savings. The Commoncog note describes effectual reasoning: begin with available means, take an affordable action, learn from it and adjust as partners or constraints become clearer. This supports a pilot designed to answer a decision-relevant question rather than one expected to validate an already fixed plan. Pearson’s curiosity essay presents interestingness as an early search signal when the eventual application is not yet known. As a counterweight to rigid portfolio scoring, it supports reserving some bounded exploration space rather than requiring every learning activity to promise immediate financial return.","url":"/stations/03-value-before-spectacle/"},{"id":"AI-04","kind":"station","number":4,"line":"A","line_name":"Strategy & Value","line_color":"#147AD6","title":"Building an agent when simpler software would do","short":"Use simpler software","summary":"Use AI where interpretation helps and ordinary software where explicit rules are sufficient.","text":"Station 04 (AI-04) Line A (Strategy & Value): Use simpler software — Building an agent when simpler software would do. Use AI where interpretation helps and ordinary software where explicit rules are sufficient. An agent-first design introduces choices and failure paths even when the task is already well specified. Conversely, forcing all ambiguous work into rigid rules can create an unmaintainable exception tree. Between rigid deterministic rules and open-ended autonomous agents lies a middle design space: a bounded contextual model call that performs a narrow semantic check or routing decision within an otherwise conventional deterministic pipeline. Split the workflow by what each step requires: retrieval, deterministic computation, interpretation, decision authority or action. Select the simplest complete system that meets the acceptance criteria. Which steps genuinely require interpretation rather than lookup, arithmetic or an explicit rule? Could a narrow contextual model check resolve the ambiguity within ordinary software before introducing autonomous planning or multi-agent loops? Can an existing service or a small workflow solve the problem without autonomous planning? Does each additional agent or tool interaction add useful capability worth its cost and failure surface? Distinguish deterministic calculation (ordinary code), narrow contextual judgment (constrained model call), open-ended planning (agentic workflow) and unresolved policy (human decision). Prototype a non-agent baseline, such as search plus a form, deterministic extraction checks or rules-based routing. Separate probabilistic interpretation from calculations, validation and authorised writes; make the boundary inspectable. Increase autonomy only when representative tests show a clear benefit over the simpler alternative, including maintenance and review effort. Tan’s supplied talk distinguishes interpretation of ambiguous intent from arithmetic, database operations and constraint handling. His examples place the latter in scripts and structured tools rather than ask language generation to be the calculation engine. Newport’s late-2025 assessment separates text-native coding environments from heterogeneous interfaces and underspecified real-world requests. The useful diagnostic questions concern hidden preferences, observable state and failures across dependent steps—not whether an agent can explain a plausible plan. The Diginomica note separates instructions, memory, execution environment, connections and the operating loop. A failed answer or action may originate in any of these components. This gives a more useful debugging structure than replacing the model before checking whether it received the right information and tools. Ismail distinguishes broad autonomous agents from narrow contextual conditionals (\"intelligence as an if-statement\"). Rather than building a multi-step planning agent or forcing ambiguous workflows into rigid boolean rules, a low-cost model can perform a single semantic check or routing judgment within conventional software. This introduces a middle design choice between brittle static rules and complex agentic coordination.","url":"/stations/04-simplest-effective-system/"},{"id":"AI-05","kind":"station","number":5,"line":"A","line_name":"Strategy & Value","line_color":"#147AD6","title":"Measuring AI activity instead of business improvement","short":"Measure outcomes","summary":"Connect usage to accepted work and accepted work to an actual business outcome.","text":"Station 05 (AI-05) Line A (Strategy & Value): Measure outcomes — Measuring AI activity instead of business improvement. Connect usage to accepted work and accepted work to an actual business outcome. Logins, tokens, drafts and generated code measure activity. They can help diagnose adoption and cost but do not establish quality, use or value. Even time saved is incomplete until the organisation understands what happens to the released capacity. Define a chain from participation to changed workflow to accepted output to the result the business or customer values. What was the pre-change baseline, and are the compared cases similar in difficulty and volume? Does “completed” mean generated, reviewed, accepted, used or resolved for the customer? Where are checking, correction, escalation and displaced downstream work included? What measured result would contradict the current claim of progress, and who is assigned and protected to present that counter-case? Choose an existing business measure, add quality and risk guardrails, and define the unit of accepted work before rollout. Collect active effort and end-to-end time separately; use a comparison design appropriate to the decision and document its limitations. Record whether released capacity becomes more throughput, better service, less overtime or a realised cost change; do not count all of these as the same benefit. Eliminate incentives and leaderboards tied to input volume (such as tokens burned, prompts submitted or seats provisioned) that reward compute consumption under Goodhart’s Law. Levie’s supplied post distinguishes compute consumption from useful results and says the answer depends on individual workflows. It is a useful challenge to an adoption dashboard: a rising activity line still needs an explanation of which business work improved. The supplied Work at the Frontier note describes people using AI for tasks beyond the conventional boundaries of their occupations. Its evidence concerns patterns in messages and occupational classification, not whether the resulting work was correct, adopted or economically valuable. The supplied Cybernetic Teammate research note reports a randomised product-innovation exercise in which AI-assisted individuals could perform comparably to unassisted teams on assessed ideas. This is a useful example of testing work quality rather than counting usage, while preserving the boundary between an exercise and sustained organisational performance. Ismail links input-heavy AI metrics to a broader decision culture in which evidence is often selected after a preferred narrative has formed, and argues that organizations rarely assign someone to develop the counter-case. In this environment, measurement dashboards routinely focus on vanity input proxies—such as token consumption, prompts submitted, or seats provisioned—rather than tracking verified changes in accepted business outcomes. Rather than relying on vague task-completion claims or raw token throughput, operational autonomy should be measured through Human Unit Review Time (HURT): the exact human minutes and attention required to review, correct, and certify an agent's deliverable. Sustainable automation economics require HURT to decline systematically over successive engagement iterations as edge cases are codified.","url":"/stations/05-measure-business-improvement/"},{"id":"AI-06","kind":"station","number":6,"line":"A","line_name":"Strategy & Value","line_color":"#147AD6","title":"Optimising model cost before proving the task works—or ignoring economics once it does","short":"Prove the economics","summary":"Establish capability inside a budget, then optimise the complete cost of successful delivery.","text":"Station 06 (AI-06) Line A (Strategy & Value): Prove the economics — Optimising model cost before proving the task works—or ignoring economics once it does. Establish capability inside a budget, then optimise the complete cost of successful delivery. A cheap model that fails does not prove a use case is infeasible, and an expensive configuration that works does not prove it is economical. Separate the feasibility question from the operating-cost question without postponing all financial discipline. Test a sufficiently capable baseline, identify why it succeeds or fails, and then compare cheaper or simpler configurations against the same quality boundary. Is the initial configuration capable enough to test the idea, or is cost minimisation masking feasibility? Which costs sit outside model tokens: retrieval, integrations, retries, human review and support? Can spending grow through loops, concurrent jobs or unexpected demand even if each request looks cheap? Allocate a bounded feasibility budget and define acceptable completion before comparing model configurations. Benchmark complete alternatives on representative work; include routing, deterministic components and escalation rather than only model substitution. Install per-task and aggregate spending limits, then revisit economics when volume, quality, prices or review requirements change. The source note links cost per successful task with internal evaluations and a system built from context, tools and instructions. This supports evaluating complete task configurations and making quality criteria explicit before interpreting a lower generation bill as progress. Acharya’s discussion calls for practical familiarity with different models and connects selection to the task’s upside and verifiability. A useful application is to compare configurations on the same accepted-work definition rather than presume the cheapest or most capable model is always the economical choice. Pearson’s editorial-workflow example distinguishes lower preparation effort from the ability to reconsider more research and produce more tailored work. Those are different benefits: one releases capacity; the other expands a feasible service. Neither should automatically be translated into cash savings. Early deployments of AI services frequently suffer from Mirage Product-Market Fit: customers are delighted by high-speed, high-quality turnarounds, but the provider is quietly subsidizing delivery with extensive uncounted human intervention and unmetered model inference. When human review and inference costs are honestly accounted for as COGS rather than overhead, gross margins collapse to 30–50% unless the core process is actively engineered toward higher autonomous completion rates. Because human thinking has historically been expensive, most operational questions and edge cases went unexamined by default. When the marginal cost of cognitive analysis plummets, organizations do not simply answer existing questions faster; demand for cognitive work expands exponentially into previously unviable investigations, personalized workflows, and continuous operational auditing. Consequently, compute and cognitive spend will surge rather than shrink.","url":"/stations/06-economics-after-feasibility/"},{"id":"AI-07","kind":"station","number":7,"line":"B","line_name":"Leadership & People","line_color":"#17884C","title":"Treating AI transformation as an IT project or one person’s responsibility","short":"Business ownership","summary":"Join executive sponsorship, process ownership and technical delivery without pretending one role can do all three.","text":"Station 07 (AI-07) Line B (Leadership & People): Business ownership — Treating AI transformation as an IT project or one person’s responsibility. Join executive sponsorship, process ownership and technical delivery without pretending one role can do all three. A technical team can build a service without having authority to resolve business priorities, change incentives or assign responsibility across departments. An executive sponsor can create urgency without owning day-to-day decisions. State which decisions sit with the sponsor, the process owner and the operational owner; give each the resources needed to carry that responsibility. Who can make cross-functional tradeoffs about the outcome, risk and workflow design? Does the named owner have authority and time, or only accountability after a failure? Which responsibilities would be uncovered if the AI specialist or sponsor left tomorrow? Name a business outcome owner, an executive escalation route and a delivery/operating owner for each initiative. Write decision rights for prioritisation, data access, acceptance, release, budget and exceptions; include affected functions rather than a ceremonial steering group. Review whether management work has genuinely disappeared or has moved into informal coordination, coaching and conflict resolution. Block’s proposed architecture includes individuals accountable for particular customer outcomes who can draw resources from several capabilities, alongside specialists and player-coaches. The useful design question is how outcome responsibility and resource authority are joined after information-routing work changes. The book’s decentralisation guidance pairs local decision-making with executive guardrails, clear decision lanes, a changed coaching role for managers and removal of cross-team blockers. Distributed work therefore does not mean that each team invents its own risk policy or loses access to shared expertise. Foss argues that firms and markets must be compared as ways to govern specific transactions. Adaptation when conditions change, relationship-specific investments and difficult-to-measure performance remain relevant even when exchanging information becomes easier. This challenges any automatic inference from AI capability to a flat organisation or universal outsourcing. The same post describes questions about cross-functional work, central management of agents, deployment, testing and ownership. Buying access leaves those decisions unresolved. It also identifies fragmented information and uneven internal skills as obstacles that a model choice alone does not address. A job bundles knowledge, time, execution, judgment, authority and responsibility. AI allows cognitive execution and research to be supplied separately, but the business remains responsible for deciding who can commit the firm and who answers for the consequences. In professional services and enterprise processes, clients and stakeholders do not merely buy cognitive output; they buy a counterparty who answers for the result.","url":"/stations/07-business-ownership/"},{"id":"AI-08","kind":"station","number":8,"line":"B","line_name":"Leadership & People","line_color":"#17884C","title":"Building a central AI team that becomes everyone’s bottleneck","short":"Avoid the bottleneck","summary":"Centralise reusable support and essential controls while keeping local outcomes and workflow knowledge close to the work.","text":"Station 08 (AI-08) Line B (Leadership & People): Avoid the bottleneck — Building a central AI team that becomes everyone’s bottleneck. Centralise reusable support and essential controls while keeping local outcomes and workflow knowledge close to the work. Central expertise is valuable, but a central team asked to discover, build, approve and support every use case becomes a queue. Unrestricted decentralisation creates a different problem: duplicated integrations, inconsistent controls and unowned applications. Distinguish shared capabilities from local delivery decisions, and make the route for obtaining help explicit. Which requests genuinely need specialist central work, and which are waiting for permission or reusable components? Can business teams build within a supported path without bypassing controls? Who maintains shared context, evaluation tools and integrations after the first team benefits? Publish a central service catalog with supported platforms, reusable patterns, review services, escalation routes and response expectations. Delegate bounded decisions to trained local owners; keep non-negotiable identity, data and release controls common. Fund maintenance of shared capabilities and recurring knowledge exchange, not only the visible backlog of new prototypes. The same post describes questions about cross-functional work, central management of agents, deployment, testing and ownership. Buying access leaves those decisions unresolved. It also identifies fragmented information and uneven internal skills as obstacles that a model choice alone does not address. The book’s decentralisation guidance pairs local decision-making with executive guardrails, clear decision lanes, a changed coaching role for managers and removal of cross-team blockers. Distributed work therefore does not mean that each team invents its own risk policy or loses access to shared expertise. The structured note describes distributed intelligence as depending on shared cross-functional context and a culture reinforced through rewards, roles and actual decisions. Granting nominal autonomy without those supports may simply relocate coordination work to overloaded local teams. Shipper describes moving from personal agents toward agents responsible for team or company jobs, with continuing engineering and maintenance. This offers a concrete explanation for why an individual’s useful experiment still needs a service owner, shared context and ongoing support before others rely on it.","url":"/stations/08-central-team-without-bottleneck/"},{"id":"AI-09","kind":"station","number":9,"line":"B","line_name":"Leadership & People","line_color":"#17884C","title":"Expecting licences and a training session to change how people work","short":"Learn in real work","summary":"Develop role-specific capability through real tasks, feedback and protected practice.","text":"Station 09 (AI-09) Line B (Leadership & People): Learn in real work — Expecting licences and a training session to change how people work. Develop role-specific capability through real tasks, feedback and protected practice. A generic course can introduce a tool without helping someone recognise a useful task, check the output or recover from a failure. Users, subject-matter experts, builders, reviewers and operators need different competencies. Training should therefore be connected to the workflow people will actually perform and the responsibility they will carry, not measured only by attendance. What must each role be able to do safely and competently after training? Do employees have approved tools, useful data, examples and time to practise on relevant work? Where can they get help when an output is plausible but wrong or a task crosses their expertise? Define practical skill expectations by role, including verification, escalation and data handling—not just prompting. Use supervised work examples, peer clinics and reusable case libraries; protect learning time in workloads. Check whether people can perform and critique representative tasks, then update the support material from recurring misunderstandings. The supplied Cybernetic Teammate research note reports a randomised product-innovation exercise in which AI-assisted individuals could perform comparably to unassisted teams on assessed ideas. This is a useful example of testing work quality rather than counting usage, while preserving the boundary between an exercise and sustained organisational performance. The supplied Work at the Frontier note describes people using AI for tasks beyond the conventional boundaries of their occupations. Its evidence concerns patterns in messages and occupational classification, not whether the resulting work was correct, adopted or economically valuable. The video-derived note distinguishes reusable skills and subject-matter intent from engineering responsibility, and calls for a governed development lifecycle for non-technical builders. This supports a division between contributing business knowledge, experimenting safely and accepting responsibility for a production service. The interview distinguishes asking worthwhile questions, selecting useful outputs and committing to action. Its execution-trap discussion warns that people can become busier producing artifacts after the bottleneck has moved elsewhere. These distinctions help specify what employees must learn and what accountable reviewers must actually do. To convert tacit delivery knowledge into durable software assets, engineering teams cannot build in isolation from specifications. AI-native services require paired engineering: embedding software engineers alongside domain practitioners who execute the actual work. Every human correction, rework cycle, and edge-case exception is captured at the operational boundary and converted directly into evaluation suites and automation logic.","url":"/stations/09-learning-in-real-work/"},{"id":"AI-10","kind":"station","number":10,"line":"B","line_name":"Leadership & People","line_color":"#17884C","title":"Letting prototypes become production systems without a governed path","short":"Govern the prototype","summary":"Give non-technical builders a supported route from useful experiment to owned, tested service.","text":"Station 10 (AI-10) Line B (Leadership & People): Govern the prototype — Letting prototypes become production systems without a governed path. Give non-technical builders a supported route from useful experiment to owned, tested service. The danger is not that a subject-matter expert creates a useful prototype. It is that a prototype gains users, permissions and business importance without a deliberate promotion decision. A production service needs someone who can explain its dependencies, evaluate changes, handle incidents and support users—even when AI generated much of the implementation. Where is the boundary between a private experiment, a shared pilot and a production dependency? Who can approve promotion, and what evidence must they inspect? Can the system be rebuilt, supported and stopped without the original creator’s personal account? Offer a sandbox and an approved promotion path with increasing controls as users, sensitivity and action authority grow. Require representative tests, named owners, service identities, versioned artifacts, support arrangements and recovery evidence before production use. Let business experts own intent and acceptance examples while involving engineering and risk specialists where the responsibility requires their skills. The video-derived note distinguishes reusable skills and subject-matter intent from engineering responsibility, and calls for a governed development lifecycle for non-technical builders. This supports a division between contributing business knowledge, experimenting safely and accepting responsibility for a production service. The addendum expands citizen development into a release decision supported by quality checks, difficult cases, security review and recovery provisions. It also separates the person who created a prototype from the person accountable for accepting it into production. The supplied 10X cheatsheet describes conventions, project specifications, active work and logs as maintained Markdown artifacts. It also distinguishes a manually asserted completion status from one derived from outstanding work. This is useful for detecting stale context and falsely complete projects.","url":"/stations/10-prototype-to-production/"},{"id":"AI-11","kind":"station","number":11,"line":"B","line_name":"Leadership & People","line_color":"#17884C","title":"Bringing Legal, Security, IT and Finance in only to approve or block the finished product","short":"Bring risk partners in","summary":"Design a workable route for responsible experimentation with the people who own its constraints.","text":"Station 11 (AI-11) Line B (Leadership & People): Bring risk partners in — Bringing Legal, Security, IT and Finance in only to approve or block the finished product. Design a workable route for responsible experimentation with the people who own its constraints. Late review turns foreseeable requirements into apparent scope creep and encourages a false choice between speed and control. Early participation is useful when it identifies the real boundary: which data, actions, costs and commitments the experiment may involve. It is not useful when every exploratory question becomes an open-ended approval queue. Which functions will need to accept the operating result, and what information do they need now? Is there an approved low-risk experimentation path with clear data and spending boundaries? Which unresolved requirements could force a redesign after users have already adopted the prototype? Hold an early use-case review with a concrete workflow and action/data map rather than a generic AI presentation. Publish supported tools, allowed data categories, spending boundaries, escalation routes and review criteria for common experiment types. Invite risk and support owners to help design acceptance and incident tests; resolve disagreements through a named business decision-maker. The source note places Legal, HR and IT involvement alongside rollout rather than as a final rejection step. The useful lesson is organisational: agree how useful experimentation can proceed and what information these teams need before a prototype becomes an operating dependency. The compiler’s expanded architecture adds identity, access policy, approval routing, evaluation, cost controls and operational oversight to a basic reasoning loop. These mechanisms belong in the surrounding service and tool interfaces; a generated instruction does not itself enforce them. Foss argues that firms and markets must be compared as ways to govern specific transactions. Adaptation when conditions change, relationship-specific investments and difficult-to-measure performance remain relevant even when exchanging information becomes easier. This challenges any automatic inference from AI capability to a flat organisation or universal outsourcing.","url":"/stations/11-risk-partners-from-start/"},{"id":"AI-12","kind":"station","number":12,"line":"B","line_name":"Leadership & People","line_color":"#17884C","title":"Treating resistance as a training problem","short":"Rebuild trust","summary":"Address trust, incentives, workload and role uncertainty rather than assume another course will solve adoption.","text":"Station 12 (AI-12) Line B (Leadership & People): Rebuild trust — Treating resistance as a training problem. Address trust, incentives, workload and role uncertainty rather than assume another course will solve adoption. People may avoid a system because it previously failed, increases checking work, threatens their status or conflicts with how performance is rewarded. They may also have legitimate concerns about confidentiality or customer impact. Find the specific obstacle before designing the intervention. Honest uncertainty about role changes is more useful than an unsupported promise that nothing will change. What happened during previous pilots, and can employees see how the current design differs? Who gains from the change and who absorbs extra work, scrutiny or uncertainty? Do incentives reward better outcomes and shared learning, or merely visible tool use and individual output? Interview users and non-users about real tasks, prior failures and perceived costs; separate training, access, design and trust problems. Explain what is known and unknown about roles, decision authority and workforce implications; involve employees in redesigning the work. Protect practice time, recognise useful corrections and make it safe to report defects or workload increases without being labelled anti-AI. Evans argues that jobs and the markets around them can change when a task becomes cheaper: demand, adjacent activities and business models also respond. This is a reason to discuss work redesign honestly without promising either that no roles will change or that a task-level capability implies a specific headcount reduction. The structured note describes distributed intelligence as depending on shared cross-functional context and a culture reinforced through rewards, roles and actual decisions. Granting nominal autonomy without those supports may simply relocate coordination work to overloaded local teams. Brooks’s supplied opinion piece raises the possibility that people use saved time to take on more work and supervise more parallel activity. It is a prompt to investigate workload, concentration and learning during rollout rather than assume faster generation automatically improves employee experience.","url":"/stations/12-resistance-incentives-trust/"},{"id":"AI-13","kind":"station","number":13,"line":"B","line_name":"Leadership & People","line_color":"#17884C","title":"Outsourcing judgement along with the task","short":"Keep your judgement","summary":"Keep the capacity to judge the work, challenge its assumptions and accept responsibility for its consequences.","text":"Station 13 (AI-13) Line B (Leadership & People): Keep your judgement — Outsourcing judgement along with the task. Keep the capacity to judge the work, challenge its assumptions and accept responsibility for its consequences. A system can produce an apparently complete answer without deciding whether the question was useful, the evidence sufficient or the action appropriate. As routine execution moves to models, humans shift from gatekeepers on the critical path to validators on the exception path—a transition familiar from the history of accounting automation. Human review fails when the reviewer lacks expertise, time, independence or authority. Furthermore, this validator model breaks down if entry-level tasks where future reviewers once learned contextual judgment are automated away without an explicit apprenticeship path. Specify the judgement that remains necessary and preserve opportunities for people to learn it; a nominal approval button does not do that work. What must a competent reviewer understand beyond the final generated output? Can the reviewer reject, change or escalate the result, and do they have time to inspect the evidence? Which learning opportunities disappear when routine work is automated, and how will junior staff develop the contextual judgment needed to validate and challenge model outputs? Define reviewer responsibilities around evidence, context, assumptions, quality and consequences; do not reduce review to stylistic editing. Provide the relevant source material and action preview, and route cases according to consequence and uncertainty rather than convenience. Build supervised practice, challenge exercises and feedback into roles so that automation does not consume the expertise needed to oversee it. The interview distinguishes asking worthwhile questions, selecting useful outputs and committing to action. Its execution-trap discussion warns that people can become busier producing artifacts after the bottleneck has moved elsewhere. These distinctions help specify what employees must learn and what accountable reviewers must actually do. The discussion explains why automating many tasks in a role does not settle the outcome: one remaining task may constrain reliability or be the service the customer values. This helps separate average task performance from the failure that makes a completed workflow unacceptable. The compiler’s critical discussion warns that agents can produce more proposed changes than experts can inspect and that codification can remove learning opportunities for future experts. A sustainable operating model must fund both genuine review and the development of people capable of performing it. The companion material describes an operating manual and a visible review surface for proposed email or customer actions. A person owns exceptional cases and approvals; the agent’s intermediate work remains inspectable. This offers a concrete alternative to an opaque personal automation. The manuscript argues that human roles shift from routine execution gatekeepers to exception validators, drawing on the historical precedent of accounting automation from physical ledgers to spreadsheets and ERP. However, it explicitly notes that automating all entry-level execution breaks the junior apprenticeship loop through which future experts historically acquired the contextual tacit knowledge needed to evaluate edge cases. Foundational AI intelligence—raw cognitive synthesis, drafting, and pattern matching—is becoming commoditized and universally available. Defensible enterprise value resides in contextual judgment: knowing client-specific trade-offs, navigating regulatory edge cases, bearing delivery risk, and maintaining human accountability for outcomes when systems fail.","url":"/stations/13-retain-judgement/"},{"id":"AI-14","kind":"station","number":14,"line":"C","line_name":"Data & Integration","line_color":"#8A2BE2","title":"Waiting for perfect data—or assuming AI will repair the data for you","short":"Fix useful data","summary":"Prepare the data needed for the chosen use case without making every project depend on a complete enterprise rebuild.","text":"Station 14 (AI-14) Line C (Data & Integration): Fix useful data — Waiting for perfect data—or assuming AI will repair the data for you. Prepare the data needed for the chosen use case without making every project depend on a complete enterprise rebuild. Two opposite assumptions create delay or failure: that all data must be cleaned before any experiment, or that a model can compensate for missing, stale and inaccessible information. Determine the minimum trustworthy input for the chosen scope. A read-only experiment may be possible before a major migration, but its source limits and the cost of adapting it later must remain visible. The manuscript offers a stronger architectural counter-view: repeated local extracts can create fragile integration, and proposes an independent governed data layer where semantics travel with the data, demoting transactional systems from data gatekeepers to consistency engines. Which specific data defects prevent this use case from meeting its acceptance criteria? Can the useful scope be narrowed while preserving permissions, freshness and a clear explanation of limitations? What changes when the source system migrates, and who will maintain the adapter or replacement? Does the extracted data carry its business semantics and governance rules with it, or will every downstream workflow have to re-engineer context? Create a use-case data contract naming required fields, definitions, coverage, freshness, access and known gaps. Repair or constrain the blocking issues first; test representative extracts before authorising consequential actions. Consider staged read-only access, a replica or an approved integration where appropriate, documenting lag, reconciliation and migration dependencies rather than assuming them away. Levie connects fragmented enterprise data with the need for company-specific context and discusses interest in multiple models. The design question is which context, tools and definitions should remain under company control when the model changes. The guide proposes moving one department at a time and stabilising its daily work before proceeding. That is an example of a bounded migration unit and adoption checkpoint. It suggests asking what must remain interoperable while old and new workflows coexist. The Reshuffle note shifts attention from automating separate tasks to assembling them into a useful service. Reducing one task’s cost may move the bottleneck to integration, trust, demand or accountable delivery. This is a reason to measure the larger outcome as well as the local improvement. The supplied analysis warns that a cited artifact may still be outdated, inapplicable or incomplete. Persistent memory also needs correction and expiry. This is especially useful for answers about exceptions: finding a previous decision should trigger an applicability check rather than automatic reuse. The manuscript argues that enterprise AI stalls primarily at the data integration layer when models are bolted onto locked transactional applications (\"group drive\"). It advocates for an independent governed data layer where semantic meaning travels bound to each object, demoting transactional engines from data gatekeepers to consistency processors. This challenges the long-term viability of relying solely on isolated, point-to-point use-case data extracts.","url":"/stations/14-use-case-data-foundation/"},{"id":"AI-15","kind":"station","number":15,"line":"C","line_name":"Data & Integration","line_color":"#8A2BE2","title":"Expecting consistent answers from inconsistent business definitions","short":"Define the terms","summary":"Make business meaning, calculation rules and source authority explicit for each question.","text":"Station 15 (AI-15) Line C (Data & Integration): Define the terms — Expecting consistent answers from inconsistent business definitions. Make business meaning, calculation rules and source authority explicit for each question. When departments use the same word differently, a model can produce a coherent answer under the wrong interpretation. The defect is not necessarily bad retrieval or arithmetic. Define the relevant entity, measure, time basis and scope, including situations where more than one definition is legitimately needed. A single “source of truth” slogan does not resolve a genuine business disagreement. Which measures have multiple valid meanings, such as orders, billings, revenue or active customers? Which reporting period, currency, status and organisational scope apply to the question? Who can approve a definition and decide how conflicts or restatements are handled? Build a small business glossary tied to real questions, calculations and source fields rather than a disconnected terminology project. Version consequential definitions and record authority, effective date and applicability; preserve legitimate alternative measures with clear names. Make the system request clarification when a material ambiguity remains, and include definition changes in regression tests. The supplied note connects reusable expertise with shared entities, synonyms, regional context and policy meaning. A semantic layer is therefore more than a dictionary: it should explain which interpretation applies in a particular business setting. The context-graphs note distinguishes operational objects and their current state from the evidence, policy, exceptions and approvals behind a decision. That distinction exposes missing context in many integrations: two identical-looking outcomes may have been produced under different rules. Tan’s talk explicitly asks what should be recorded, how it should be linked, what stays in active memory and who resolves contradictions. These questions are useful even without adopting his particular personal system: retrieval cannot choose the organisation’s authority hierarchy for it. Ajzenstadt’s forecast-variance example requires agreement about forecasts, actuals, thresholds and who receives the result. Some of the apparent implementation work is therefore deciding how the business should operate. An agent cannot reliably recover a policy that the organisation has never agreed.","url":"/stations/15-shared-business-definitions/"},{"id":"AI-16","kind":"station","number":16,"line":"C","line_name":"Data & Integration","line_color":"#8A2BE2","title":"Treating an available data feed as the whole picture","short":"Know the blind spots","summary":"Make the boundary of the evidence visible: coverage, missingness, history and freshness.","text":"Station 16 (AI-16) Line C (Data & Integration): Know the blind spots — Treating an available data feed as the whole picture. Make the boundary of the evidence visible: coverage, missingness, history and freshness. An answer can be correct for a source but misleading for the business. The source may exclude certain customers, historical periods, events or fields. Absence in an extract does not establish absence in reality. Treat the feed’s scope as part of the answer and define when insufficient coverage requires abstention, clarification or another data source. Which customers, regions, products and periods are not represented? Are missing values genuine negatives, unavailable fields, failed ingestion or a permissions effect? Can the user tell what the answer includes, excludes and assumes about freshness? Publish a coverage manifest for each source and connect it to the retrieval or answering workflow. Test known-absent, partially covered and stale cases; require the system to distinguish “not found here” from “does not exist.” Monitor ingestion and schema changes so that coverage loss becomes an operational event rather than an invisible change in answers. Sphere’s supplied note distinguishes expertise itself from the artifacts left when experts act. It describes retrieving evidence, returning cited answers and having experts validate them. That is useful when institutional knowledge is dispersed across past work, while preserving the difference between a trace and complete understanding. The supplied analysis warns that a cited artifact may still be outdated, inapplicable or incomplete. Persistent memory also needs correction and expiry. This is especially useful for answers about exceptions: finding a previous decision should trigger an applicability check rather than automatic reuse. The compiler’s risk analysis identifies policy drift, incomplete capture, biased precedent and poorly governed reuse as dangers in decision histories. A prior exception should remain tied to its scope and authority; it should not silently become the default rule for later cases. The supplied Work at the Frontier note describes people using AI for tasks beyond the conventional boundaries of their occupations. Its evidence concerns patterns in messages and occupational classification, not whether the resulting work was correct, adopted or economically valuable.","url":"/stations/16-data-coverage-and-limits/"},{"id":"AI-17","kind":"station","number":17,"line":"C","line_name":"Data & Integration","line_color":"#8A2BE2","title":"Leaving essential business rules in people’s heads","short":"Capture the know-how","summary":"Capture decision-relevant expertise while preserving the people and practice that keep it current.","text":"Station 17 (AI-17) Line C (Data & Integration): Capture the know-how — Leaving essential business rules in people’s heads. Capture decision-relevant expertise while preserving the people and practice that keep it current. A process can depend on an experienced colleague’s recognition of exceptions that never appear in a manual. Capturing this knowledge requires cases, reasoning and applicability—not just recording a meeting. Elicitation also faces trust barriers: specialists hesitate to codify tacit expertise if they anticipate it diminishes their future standing. Runtime decision traces can complement static documentation by recording how real exceptions were resolved (inputs, policy invoked, approver, precedent). Assign stewardship and preserve opportunities for future experts to learn through supervised work. Which decisions repeatedly require a particular person, and what makes those cases unusual? Can experts explain the evidence, tradeoffs and circumstances under which a rule should not apply? Who validates, updates or retires captured knowledge after the original expert leaves? Do specialists have career security and incentives to codify their tacit knowledge, or does sharing expertise threaten their future standing? Prioritise consequential, recurring decisions and interview experts using real contrasting cases rather than abstract requests to document everything. Store the reasoning, sources, authority, exceptions, scope and known limits with each reusable rule; obtain expert review. Attach ownership, review triggers and regression cases, while maintaining apprenticeship and a route for new evidence to challenge established practice. The CMR source note describes identifying critical expertise and making context-dependent reasoning visible through cases and exceptions. The practical opportunity is to prioritise knowledge that repeatedly changes important decisions, rather than launch an indiscriminate document-everything programme. The CMR note gives people an ongoing role in checking cases, challenging outputs and maintaining business relevance. Capturing an expert’s reasoning is the start of a stewardship process, not evidence that the expert’s role or the need for future learning has disappeared. Sphere’s supplied note distinguishes expertise itself from the artifacts left when experts act. It describes retrieving evidence, returning cited answers and having experts validate them. That is useful when institutional knowledge is dispersed across past work, while preserving the difference between a trace and complete understanding. The notes describe comparing accumulated guidance with advice given in real interactions. That is useful for identifying outdated procedure and exceptions, but the observed advice still needs review before becoming a general rule. Recording more interactions also raises consent and access questions. Rather than attempting to extract expert knowledge into static manuals, the manuscript proposes capturing runtime decision traces whenever an expert resolves an ambiguous exception (inputs gathered, policy invoked, approver identity, precedent). The manuscript also highlights a contested counterargument, attributed to Alloy Partners, that aggressive delayering can remove tacit translation and cross-functional integration work whose loss may only become visible later, echoing the delayed innovation deficits of 1990s corporate reengineering.","url":"/stations/17-capture-and-renew-expertise/"},{"id":"AI-18","kind":"station","number":18,"line":"C","line_name":"Data & Integration","line_color":"#8A2BE2","title":"Making the model read everything to find one thing","short":"Retrieve what matters","summary":"Provide permission-aware retrieval that returns the right evidence and makes retrieval failures diagnosable.","text":"Station 18 (AI-18) Line C (Data & Integration): Retrieve what matters — Making the model read everything to find one thing. Provide permission-aware retrieval that returns the right evidence and makes retrieval failures diagnosable. A large context window does not decide what evidence matters, whether the correct source was found or which user may see it. Give the model tools suited to the task: search, filtering, structured queries and progressive detail. Evaluate retrieval independently enough to tell whether an incorrect answer came from missing evidence or misuse of evidence that was available. Can the system retrieve the authoritative record without receiving irrelevant or unauthorised material? Do retrieved results carry source identity, version, date and enough surrounding context to interpret them? Are known retrieval failures represented in tests, including conflicting, missing and access-restricted evidence? Design task-specific search and filtering tools with explicit input/output contracts and enforced permissions. Return relevant passages or records with provenance and enough context to preserve qualifications; allow deeper retrieval when needed. Test retrieval coverage and answer grounding separately, then trace both through end-to-end acceptance cases. The Diginomica note separates instructions, memory, execution environment, connections and the operating loop. A failed answer or action may originate in any of these components. This gives a more useful debugging structure than replacing the model before checking whether it received the right information and tools. The supplied analysis warns that a cited artifact may still be outdated, inapplicable or incomplete. Persistent memory also needs correction and expiry. This is especially useful for answers about exceptions: finding a previous decision should trigger an applicability check rather than automatic reuse. Tan’s talk explicitly asks what should be recorded, how it should be linked, what stays in active memory and who resolves contradictions. These questions are useful even without adopting his particular personal system: retrieval cannot choose the organisation’s authority hierarchy for it.","url":"/stations/18-relevant-retrieval/"},{"id":"AI-19","kind":"station","number":19,"line":"C","line_name":"Data & Integration","line_color":"#8A2BE2","title":"Assuming connected systems will work together correctly","short":"Test integrations","summary":"Test business semantics and partial effects, not just successful API connections.","text":"Station 19 (AI-19) Line C (Data & Integration): Test integrations — Assuming connected systems will work together correctly. Test business semantics and partial effects, not just successful API connections. A tool can connect successfully while acting on the wrong identity, unit, period or record. Multi-step work adds the possibility that one action succeeds and the next fails. Define how state moves between systems, how retries are recognised and how discrepancies are reconciled. The contract must describe business meaning and failure behaviour as well as technical fields. How are identities, versions, units, currencies and status meanings matched between systems? What happens if the response times out after an action has already succeeded? Can partial completion, duplicate events and out-of-order updates be detected and repaired? Document each tool’s allowed effects, preconditions, success evidence and failure states; keep calculations and validations explicit. Use suitable duplicate-prevention and reconciliation mechanisms for consequential writes, then test timeouts and retries in a safe environment. Validate complete real workflows across source and destination systems, including permission boundaries and downstream acceptance. The Diginomica note separates instructions, memory, execution environment, connections and the operating loop. A failed answer or action may originate in any of these components. This gives a more useful debugging structure than replacing the model before checking whether it received the right information and tools. The supplied failure dossier includes unsafe retries, partial transactions, duplicate actions, poisoned context, weak identity boundaries and missing recovery. These are useful test categories because a superficially successful response can coexist with a damaged record or an action executed twice. Newport’s late-2025 assessment separates text-native coding environments from heterogeneous interfaces and underspecified real-world requests. The useful diagnostic questions concern hidden preferences, observable state and failures across dependent steps—not whether an agent can explain a plausible plan.","url":"/stations/19-integration-behaviour/"},{"id":"AI-20","kind":"station","number":20,"line":"D","line_name":"Security & Control","line_color":"#D63B2F","title":"Confusing instructions with access controls","short":"Enforce permissions","summary":"Enforce what an agent may read and do in software, using identities and permissions appropriate to its job.","text":"Station 20 (AI-20) Line D (Security & Control): Enforce permissions — Confusing instructions with access controls. Enforce what an agent may read and do in software, using identities and permissions appropriate to its job. A prompt expresses an intended behaviour; it does not remove a capability from the surrounding system. Scope the agent’s actual access, distinguish the requesting user from the service identity and make consequential actions inspectable and attributable. Separate read, write, send, delete and spending authority rather than treating a connector as a single undifferentiated permission. What can the agent actually do with its current credentials, regardless of its instructions? Whose authority is represented by each action, and can it exceed the requesting user’s permitted scope? Can access be revoked or narrowed without relying on the agent to cooperate? Create a capability matrix by task and identity; grant only the data and actions needed for the approved job. Enforce permissions at the service and tool boundary, with appropriate short-lived credentials and approval for consequential actions. Test denied actions and cross-user cases; keep an attributable record of the requester, agent identity, approved scope and executed effect. The compiler’s expanded architecture adds identity, access policy, approval routing, evaluation, cost controls and operational oversight to a basic reasoning loop. These mechanisms belong in the surrounding service and tool interfaces; a generated instruction does not itself enforce them. The video-derived note sets the requesting user’s permissions as an upper bound. The compiler’s addendum separates that human authority from the service identity used by the agent. Together these suggest an implementation with attributable execution and enforcement that the agent does not silently exceed the requester’s allowed scope.","url":"/stations/20-enforced-agent-permissions/"},{"id":"AI-21","kind":"station","number":21,"line":"D","line_name":"Security & Control","line_color":"#D63B2F","title":"Making data protection depend on employees remembering the rules","short":"Protect data by design","summary":"Make approved data handling understandable and supported by technical controls across the information lifecycle.","text":"Station 21 (AI-21) Line D (Security & Control): Protect data by design — Making data protection depend on employees remembering the rules. Make approved data handling understandable and supported by technical controls across the information lifecycle. Data protection can fail at input, retrieval, output, logs, persistent memory or sharing—not only when someone forgets to remove a name. Identify the permitted purpose and audience at each stage. Account settings and contracts matter, but they need to be connected to an approved working path that people can actually use. Which services and accounts receive company information, and who has checked their permitted use and retention arrangements? Can sensitive information move from a legitimate read into an inappropriate output, trace or shared memory? Are collection, access, retention, correction and deletion responsibilities assigned? Classify the data and approved purposes for each workflow; provide supported accounts and connectors rather than rely on informal personal use. Apply suitable access restrictions and automated input/output checks, and minimise what is retained in traces and memory. Define retention and deletion processes with responsible specialists, then test the real data path—including logs, cached copies and downstream destinations. The addendum discusses information lifecycle and authority to edit, approve or retire shared context. A company can have accurate retrieval and still produce bad answers when an unreviewed definition or obsolete instruction is promoted into its common knowledge layer. The compiler’s risk analysis identifies policy drift, incomplete capture, biased precedent and poorly governed reuse as dangers in decision histories. A prior exception should remain tied to its scope and authority; it should not silently become the default rule for later cases. Tan’s talk explicitly asks what should be recorded, how it should be linked, what stays in active memory and who resolves contradictions. These questions are useful even without adopting his particular personal system: retrieval cannot choose the organisation’s authority hierarchy for it.","url":"/stations/21-data-protection-by-design/"},{"id":"AI-22","kind":"station","number":22,"line":"D","line_name":"Security & Control","line_color":"#D63B2F","title":"Trusting every instruction an agent encounters","short":"Distrust outside instructions","summary":"Treat retrieved documents, messages and tool results as evidence to inspect—not as authority to change the agent’s rules.","text":"Station 22 (AI-22) Line D (Security & Control): Distrust outside instructions — Trusting every instruction an agent encounters. Treat retrieved documents, messages and tool results as evidence to inspect—not as authority to change the agent’s rules. An agent may encounter text that asks it to ignore instructions, disclose information or call a tool. Such text can be part of the task data rather than an authorised command. Design for that distinction and enforce the action boundary outside the model. Prompt wording can help communicate intent but should not be the only defence. Can material from a customer, website, attachment or retrieved record influence tool use beyond its authorised role? Which controls still hold if the model follows a malicious or misleading instruction in that material? Can a contaminated memory or shared instruction file affect future users or workflows? Identify untrusted input surfaces and keep them separate from approved policy and task instructions in the system design. Use constrained tools, enforced permissions and explicit approval for high-consequence effects; do not allow encountered text to grant new access. Test realistic redirection and data-exfiltration attempts in an authorised test environment, including stored-context contamination and cross-document instructions. The compiler’s security analysis includes prompt injection and compromised memory or context. The practical distinction is between content the agent must examine and instructions entitled to govern its actions. Tools should not accept a permission change merely because a document asks the agent to make one. The compiler’s expanded architecture adds identity, access policy, approval routing, evaluation, cost controls and operational oversight to a basic reasoning loop. These mechanisms belong in the surrounding service and tool interfaces; a generated instruction does not itself enforce them. The compiler’s risk analysis identifies policy drift, incomplete capture, biased precedent and poorly governed reuse as dangers in decision histories. A prior exception should remain tied to its scope and authority; it should not silently become the default rule for later cases.","url":"/stations/22-untrusted-instructions/"},{"id":"AI-23","kind":"station","number":23,"line":"D","line_name":"Security & Control","line_color":"#D63B2F","title":"Letting agents run or spend without hard limits","short":"Set hard limits","summary":"Bound the amount of work, money and external effect an agent can authorise before the system stops or seeks approval.","text":"Station 23 (AI-23) Line D (Security & Control): Set hard limits — Letting agents run or spend without hard limits. Bound the amount of work, money and external effect an agent can authorise before the system stops or seeks approval. A per-request price can hide an unbounded loop, repeated retries or many concurrent jobs. Spending exposure also includes payments, subscriptions and external commitments, not only model usage. Define aggregate limits and the behaviour at the boundary before deployment. The stopping mechanism must be enforceable by the host service, not dependent on the agent remembering its budget. What is the maximum aggregate exposure across jobs, retries, teams and connected paid services? Who can change a limit, and is that change recorded and separately authorised? What does the system return or do when time, requests, money or action limits are reached? Set hard limits for the relevant dimensions: runtime, requests, retries, concurrency, paid tools and transactions; include aggregate budgets. Choose a safe boundary behaviour such as pause, partial result or approval request, and ensure it does not create uncontrolled follow-on work. Test exhaustion and runaway scenarios, alert accountable owners and retain enough evidence to understand where resources went. The compiler’s expanded architecture adds identity, access policy, approval routing, evaluation, cost controls and operational oversight to a basic reasoning loop. These mechanisms belong in the surrounding service and tool interfaces; a generated instruction does not itself enforce them. Rao’s conceptual distinction concerns whether acting generates usable information or instead outruns feedback and creates external costs. Applied to an agent, the question is how much it can change before the organisation can observe, interpret and contain an error.","url":"/stations/23-bounded-agent-execution/"},{"id":"AI-24","kind":"station","number":24,"line":"D","line_name":"Security & Control","line_color":"#D63B2F","title":"Applying the same controls to every use case","short":"Match controls to risk","summary":"Calibrate autonomy and assurance to each decision’s consequences, uncertainty and reversibility.","text":"Station 24 (AI-24) Line D (Security & Control): Match controls to risk — Applying the same controls to every use case. Calibrate autonomy and assurance to each decision’s consequences, uncertainty and reversibility. A drafting tool and a system that changes a customer’s entitlement do not expose people to the same consequences. Even within one workflow, a read-only step and an irreversible action may need different controls. Avoid collapsing task autonomy, user collaboration and organisational readiness into a single maturity score. Assess who can be affected and what recourse exists. Who could be harmed by an incorrect or unfair result, and can the effect be reversed in practice? Which actions are genuinely reversible two-way doors suitable for automated execution, and which are one-way fiduciary commitments that must never execute without human sign-off? Which decisions require stronger evidence, specialist review or an opportunity to challenge the outcome? Can the organisation actually monitor and support the level of autonomy being proposed? Classify the workflow’s decisions by consequence, sensitivity, uncertainty, affected population and reversibility, using specialists where needed. Implement graduated authority envelopes where agents earn expanded operational discretion based on demonstrated performance telemetry, while reserving irreversible commitments under a strict fiduciary boundary requiring accountable human sign-off. Choose task-specific control patterns: assistance, reviewed proposal, constrained execution or stronger approval; make prohibited uses explicit. Test relevant quality, fairness, misuse and recovery concerns, and provide a route for affected users to obtain correction or human review. The discussion explains why automating many tasks in a role does not settle the outcome: one remaining task may constrain reliability or be the service the customer values. This helps separate average task performance from the failure that makes a completed workflow unacceptable. The manuscript outlines graduated authority: systems earn expanded operational discretion based on demonstrated performance telemetry rather than static permission grants. Consequential and irreversible commitments (financial disbursements, legal agreements, external disclosures) remain bound by a 'fiduciary wedge' requiring accountable human sign-off, whereas routine reversible actions operate under confidence-scored exception routing.","url":"/stations/24-controls-proportionate-to-risk/"},{"id":"AI-25","kind":"station","number":25,"line":"E","line_name":"Evaluation & Delivery","line_color":"#E8AF10","title":"Building before agreeing what a good result looks like","short":"Define good results","summary":"Turn business expectations into representative cases, acceptance criteria and boundaries for abstention.","text":"Station 25 (AI-25) Line E (Evaluation & Delivery): Define good results — Building before agreeing what a good result looks like. Turn business expectations into representative cases, acceptance criteria and boundaries for abstention. Without an agreed standard, a team can improve fluent output while disagreeing about whether it is useful or safe. Start with real work and the people who can judge it. Define unacceptable failures and when the correct response is to ask for help. Preserve verified corrections as tests, while avoiding a test set that becomes only a record of past successes. Can domain experts agree on why a result is acceptable, unacceptable or requires clarification? Do the examples reflect the actual range of tasks and important exceptions? What happens to a verified correction after the immediate incident is resolved? Collect representative cases with source evidence, expected behaviour and a rationale; involve business experts in resolving disagreement. Define acceptance at the workflow level, including actions, abstention, authority and downstream use—not only answer style. Version the evaluation set and turn verified failures into regression cases; keep separate cases for development and for independent checks where practical. Every’s companion workflow notes recommend writing evaluation cases before relying on long-running agents, including situations that should not trigger the skill. The retained artifact is not just a better prompt but trigger rules, realistic cases and reusable lessons from verified failures. The twelve-factor note includes evaluation rubrics, retained operational traces and encoded expertise as parts of a learning system. Together they suggest that trace collection is useful only when the organisation can interpret outcomes and revise a tested process. The CMR note gives people an ongoing role in checking cases, challenging outputs and maintaining business relevance. Capturing an expert’s reasoning is the start of a stewardship process, not evidence that the expert’s role or the need for future learning has disappeared.","url":"/stations/25-define-good-results/"},{"id":"AI-26","kind":"station","number":26,"line":"E","line_name":"Evaluation & Delivery","line_color":"#E8AF10","title":"Letting the builders test only the happy paths","short":"Test the hard cases","summary":"Use domain users, exceptions and adversarial cases to test the boundaries of the entire workflow.","text":"Station 26 (AI-26) Line E (Evaluation & Delivery): Test the hard cases — Letting the builders test only the happy paths. Use domain users, exceptions and adversarial cases to test the boundaries of the entire workflow. Builders know the intended path and may unconsciously supply missing context or avoid awkward requests. Real work includes ambiguity, unusual units, unavailable tools, stale rules and requests that should not be executed. Bring people who understand the business consequences into the test process, and test correct refusal and escalation as first-class outcomes. Which cases would an experienced employee hesitate over or send to someone else? Have missing evidence, conflicting rules, denied access, partial completion and unusual units been tested? Can testers challenge assumptions independently of the people who designed the system? Recruit representative domain users and independent reviewers; ask them to supply difficult real cases and explain why they matter. Create a risk-based coverage matrix spanning normal, ambiguous, exceptional, denied and adversarial situations; avoid relying only on random easy examples. Test both the output and actual tool effects, including stop/ask-for-help behaviour; preserve failures with verified expected results. Every’s companion workflow notes recommend writing evaluation cases before relying on long-running agents, including situations that should not trigger the skill. The retained artifact is not just a better prompt but trigger rules, realistic cases and reusable lessons from verified failures. The supplied failure dossier includes unsafe retries, partial transactions, duplicate actions, poisoned context, weak identity boundaries and missing recovery. These are useful test categories because a superficially successful response can coexist with a damaged record or an action executed twice. The compiler’s security analysis includes prompt injection and compromised memory or context. The practical distinction is between content the agent must examine and instructions entitled to govern its actions. Tools should not accept a permission change merely because a document asks the agent to make one. The discussion explains why automating many tasks in a role does not settle the outcome: one remaining task may constrain reliability or be the service the customer values. This helps separate average task performance from the failure that makes a completed workflow unacceptable.","url":"/stations/26-test-difficult-and-adversarial-cases/"},{"id":"AI-27","kind":"station","number":27,"line":"E","line_name":"Evaluation & Delivery","line_color":"#E8AF10","title":"Blaming or changing the model without checking the whole system","short":"Debug the whole system","summary":"Diagnose model, context, retrieval, tool and orchestration failures separately, then evaluate the complete configuration.","text":"Station 27 (AI-27) Line E (Evaluation & Delivery): Debug the whole system — Blaming or changing the model without checking the whole system. Diagnose model, context, retrieval, tool and orchestration failures separately, then evaluate the complete configuration. The visible symptom appears in the agent’s answer or action, but the cause may be an obsolete rule, wrong source, malformed tool response or orchestration error. A model change can hide the immediate symptom while leaving the underlying defect. Capture enough traceable evidence to reproduce the failure and test the component hypothesis before choosing a remedy. Did the system receive the right task, authorised evidence and current business rules? Were tool calls, responses and state transitions correct, and did the model use them appropriately? Can the failing case be reproduced against the exact prior configuration? Record the model, instructions, context and tool versions together with permitted task traces and outcomes. Classify failures by mechanism, form a testable diagnosis and change one relevant element at a time where feasible. Rerun the failed cases and broader acceptance suite after any important change, including apparently minor connector or source updates. The Diginomica note separates instructions, memory, execution environment, connections and the operating loop. A failed answer or action may originate in any of these components. This gives a more useful debugging structure than replacing the model before checking whether it received the right information and tools. The supplied analysis explains that model choice and agent-environment choice are different decisions. Provider-specific behaviour and integration assumptions can remain even behind a common interface. The organisation should test the complete replacement configuration, not infer portability from the connection code alone. The supplied YC example describes a data-query agent and the use of failed attempts to improve its future operation. The key pattern is the return path from a real task to a checked correction—not the assumption that recording usage automatically teaches the system what was right. Newport’s late-2025 assessment separates text-native coding environments from heterogeneous interfaces and underspecified real-world requests. The useful diagnostic questions concern hidden preferences, observable state and failures across dependent steps—not whether an agent can explain a plausible plan.","url":"/stations/27-debug-the-whole-system/"},{"id":"AI-28","kind":"station","number":28,"line":"E","line_name":"Evaluation & Delivery","line_color":"#E8AF10","title":"Generating work faster than people can check and use it","short":"Plan review capacity","summary":"Plan the capacity to review, accept and use output—not just the capacity to generate it.","text":"Station 28 (AI-28) Line E (Evaluation & Delivery): Plan review capacity — Generating work faster than people can check and use it. Plan the capacity to review, accept and use output—not just the capacity to generate it. Faster generation can move the constraint into testing, expert review, publication or customer adoption: individual tasks run minutes faster, yet total end-to-end cycle time remains flat because work waits in the same approval queues. In Ju’s classification, monotonic tasks can execute without coordination for correctness, whereas non-monotonic tasks still require coordination; this formal property does not determine whether human judgment or coordination for quality remains useful. Measure the queue from proposal to accepted use and decide which checking can be automated, which needs expertise and how demand should be limited while that capacity is developed. Where does generated work wait, and how much is discarded or rewritten before use? Do reviewers have time and evidence to detect the failures they are responsible for catching? Has faster drafting reduced the end-to-end cycle time to customer delivery, or merely relocated waiting work into an overloaded approval queue? Has automation removed the practice through which future reviewers would learn? Map the acceptance path and review capacity before increasing generation volume; include downstream use and release gates. Automate suitable objective checks, reduce unnecessary output and prioritise work by value rather than pushing every artifact into a human queue. Staff and protect expert review, maintain supervised learning opportunities and throttle generation when meaningful inspection cannot keep pace. The essay describes how more accessible execution changes the work of deciding what matters, judging the result and carrying responsibility. This is useful for capacity planning: additional drafts or code are not automatically additional accepted output when review, prioritisation or distribution remains constrained. The compiler’s critical discussion warns that agents can produce more proposed changes than experts can inspect and that codification can remove learning opportunities for future experts. A sustainable operating model must fund both genuine review and the development of people capable of performing it. The interview distinguishes asking worthwhile questions, selecting useful outputs and committing to action. Its execution-trap discussion warns that people can become busier producing artifacts after the bottleneck has moved elsewhere. These distinctions help specify what employees must learn and what accountable reviewers must actually do. Brooks’s supplied opinion piece raises the possibility that people use saved time to take on more work and supervise more parallel activity. It is a prompt to investigate workload, concentration and learning during rollout rather than assume faster generation automatically improves employee experience. Ismail points out that accelerating individual tasks can leave total organizational cycle time unchanged when work continues to wait in existing human approval and review queues. While tracking human override rates over time can indicate operational learning, a declining override rate must be evaluated against reviewer fatigue, rubber-stamping, and escaped defects. Ju applies the distributed systems CALM theorem to organizational tasks, demonstrating that coordination mechanisms are required for correctness only when tasks are non-monotonic (where new information can invalidate prior conclusions). Across 65 APQC enterprise workflows, 48 (74%) were classified as monotonic and provably executable without coordination, with non-monotonic workflows clustering around shared finite resources such as budgets, headcounts, and physical capacity. Replicating across 13,417 O*NET occupational tasks yielded a 42% monotonic rate. When cognitive work becomes cheap, the set of worthwhile investigations expands, but so does the volume of unneeded analysis. The critical management filter is whether autonomous investigation changes a consequential decision or improves a measurable outcome, rather than evaluating agents by the sheer volume of generated briefs or reports.","url":"/stations/28-review-capacity-and-throughput/"},{"id":"AI-29","kind":"station","number":29,"line":"E","line_name":"Evaluation & Delivery","line_color":"#E8AF10","title":"Calling a demo production-ready—or estimating “90% done” by counting features","short":"Go beyond the demo","summary":"Estimate remaining uncertainty and operational work rather than the fraction of visible features built.","text":"Station 29 (AI-29) Line E (Evaluation & Delivery): Go beyond the demo — Calling a demo production-ready—or estimating “90% done” by counting features. Estimate remaining uncertainty and operational work rather than the fraction of visible features built. A polished demonstration can avoid the workflows most likely to fail in production. Feature counts conceal unresolved integrations, rare exceptions, review capacity, deployment and support. Treat readiness as evidence about real operating conditions, not a percentage inferred from the easiest completed screens or tasks. Make the remaining unknowns visible before users depend on the service. Which difficult workflows, integration states and user behaviours remain untested? What support, monitoring, recovery and access work is absent from the feature list? Does the stated completion status follow from verified evidence or from a manual assertion? Identify high-uncertainty and high-consequence workflows early and test them before polishing the easy path. Use a readiness checklist covering acceptance, identity, monitoring, support, rollback and live-system constraints; name the release decision owner. Estimate remaining work from unresolved risks and dependencies, and show what is unproven rather than publish a misleading feature percentage. The addendum expands citizen development into a release decision supported by quality checks, difficult cases, security review and recovery provisions. It also separates the person who created a prototype from the person accountable for accepting it into production. The supplied 10X cheatsheet describes conventions, project specifications, active work and logs as maintained Markdown artifacts. It also distinguishes a manually asserted completion status from one derived from outstanding work. This is useful for detecting stale context and falsely complete projects. Newport’s late-2025 assessment separates text-native coding environments from heterogeneous interfaces and underspecified real-world requests. The useful diagnostic questions concern hidden preferences, observable state and failures across dependent steps—not whether an agent can explain a plausible plan. Traditional professional services firms suffer from linear economics where revenue growth requires proportional headcount expansion. AI-native services achieve non-linear leverage by channeling delivery through proprietary platforms that capture workflow metadata, edge-case resolutions, and systems of record. As the platform's autonomous completion rate rises, gross margins expand from services levels (30–50%) toward software economics (70%+).","url":"/stations/29-production-readiness-not-feature-count/"},{"id":"AI-30","kind":"station","number":30,"line":"E","line_name":"Evaluation & Delivery","line_color":"#E8AF10","title":"Running pilots without decisions, or committing to plans that cannot change","short":"Decide on pilots","summary":"Give each experiment a decision and keep the programme able to act on what it learns.","text":"Station 30 (AI-30) Line E (Evaluation & Delivery): Decide on pilots — Running pilots without decisions, or committing to plans that cannot change. Give each experiment a decision and keep the programme able to act on what it learns. An endless pilot consumes attention without committing to adoption or closure. A rigid programme can create the opposite failure: evidence arrives but cannot change the roadmap. Maintain a durable business direction while allowing experiments to alter scope, architecture and priorities. Define what would count as useful learning and who has authority to act on it. What decision will this pilot enable, and when will that decision be made? Which evidence would cause the team to stop, narrow the scope or choose a different approach, and who is institutionally protected to surface that counter-case? What must be funded and owned for a successful pilot to become sustained use? State the hypothesis, uncertainty, resource boundary, acceptance evidence and scale/adapt/stop decision before the pilot starts. Review results with business users and operating owners, including quality, adoption burden and integration effort—not only technical feasibility. Keep a visible record of changed assumptions and retired work; require a new decision when conditions or system behaviour materially change. The Commoncog note describes effectual reasoning: begin with available means, take an affordable action, learn from it and adjust as partners or constraints become clearer. This supports a pilot designed to answer a decision-relevant question rather than one expected to validate an already fixed plan. Ismail proposes an isolated AI-enabled counterpart of a predictable workflow, compared with the existing process before broader migration. The useful element is the protected comparison and staged decision; it does not require accepting the wider forecast about the future shape of companies. Pearson’s curiosity essay presents interestingness as an early search signal when the eventual application is not yet known. As a counterweight to rigid portfolio scoring, it supports reserving some bounded exploration space rather than requiring every learning activity to promise immediate financial return. The essay contrasts well-bounded, replayable environments with open organisational settings in which objectives conflict and state is incomplete. A locally successful output can still create work or harm elsewhere. Evaluation should therefore seek feedback from the people receiving the result, not only from the producing agent. Ismail argues that major organizational decisions routinely lack an explicit role protected to surface disconfirming evidence, allowing narrative momentum to override contradictory facts. In pilot governance, this suggests that formal stopping criteria remain vulnerable unless someone is institutionally protected to present the case against continuing.","url":"/stations/30-pilots-with-decisions/"},{"id":"AI-31","kind":"station","number":31,"line":"F","line_name":"Operations & Scaling","line_color":"#078C9B","title":"Buying external help without clear deliverables, ownership or an exit plan","short":"Plan the handover","summary":"Buy a defined outcome and the ability to operate it, not indefinite dependence on the original supplier.","text":"Station 31 (AI-31) Line F (Operations & Scaling): Plan the handover — Buying external help without clear deliverables, ownership or an exit plan. Buy a defined outcome and the ability to operate it, not indefinite dependence on the original supplier. Discovery and implementation can both be worthwhile, but they need different deliverables. A consultant may help identify uncertainty without promising a production build; a production supplier must leave an operable system. Establish what the company will receive, control and be able to maintain before the relationship becomes essential. A handover document without access or tested rebuildability is not enough. What tangible evidence or decision must discovery deliver before more work is authorised? Which code, configuration, context, tests, data and operating records will the company control? Could another competent team diagnose and operate the service without the supplier’s private accounts or undocumented knowledge? Separate discovery, pilot and production commitments with explicit deliverables, acceptance criteria and decision points. Agree responsibilities for documentation, access, support, updates, incident response and the relevant usage/ownership rights with appropriate procurement and legal support. Test handover: have a different operator reproduce a deployment, run evaluations and perform a recovery exercise using the supplied materials. The expanded note identifies policies, company context, evaluations, tool semantics and operational records as assets to own or contractually control. The point is practical continuity: another competent team needs enough information and access to run, diagnose and change the system. Foss applies the make-or-buy question to AI itself: company-specific knowledge and integrations can create dependence on a provider even as technical access becomes easier. Procurement should examine adaptation, control and switching costs rather than compare the initial service fee alone. In modern transformation delivery, selling tooling licenses ('copilots') is replaced by selling completed working processes ('autopilots'). Delivery compounds across engagements only when expert human corrections are turned into reusable methods and test rubrics that survive outside the original assignment, leaving the client with an operating process their own team can run and improve.","url":"/stations/31-supplier-deliverables-and-handover/"},{"id":"AI-32","kind":"station","number":32,"line":"F","line_name":"Operations & Scaling","line_color":"#078C9B","title":"Choosing a provider without understanding the cost of changing it","short":"Keep switching feasible","summary":"Understand where dependence lives and test a realistic exit path without overengineering portability.","text":"Station 32 (AI-32) Line F (Operations & Scaling): Keep switching feasible — Choosing a provider without understanding the cost of changing it. Understand where dependence lives and test a realistic exit path without overengineering portability. A standard API can make a technical connection easy to replace while leaving prompts, tools, evaluations, data formats and commercial rights tied to one provider. Conversely, an elaborate provider-neutral platform may cost more than the flexibility it creates. Beyond model interfaces and prompts, an additional switching cost can emerge from the accumulated event log and runtime decision traces needed to reproduce domain behavior. Identify the dependencies that matter for continuity, bargaining power and future improvement, then test the replacement effort that is actually relevant. Which assets and behaviours depend on the current provider rather than on the business task itself? Can the company export and use the context, configurations, evaluations and records needed to reproduce the service? Does the organisation own and have the right to extract the complete append-only log of agent actions, context and decisions, or does operational history remain captive to the provider? What evidence would justify switching rather than remaining with an adequate existing solution? Maintain a dependency map covering technical interfaces, behavioural assumptions, data access, contracts and operational expertise. Keep business definitions, evaluations and critical context under suitable control, and test an alternative configuration on representative tasks when the exposure warrants it. Make switching decisions against expected future value, migration cost and risk—not sunk spending or model-release excitement. The supplied analysis explains that model choice and agent-environment choice are different decisions. Provider-specific behaviour and integration assumptions can remain even behind a common interface. The organisation should test the complete replacement configuration, not infer portability from the connection code alone. Foss applies the make-or-buy question to AI itself: company-specific knowledge and integrations can create dependence on a provider even as technical access becomes easier. Procurement should examine adaptation, control and switching costs rather than compare the initial service fee alone. Levie connects fragmented enterprise data with the need for company-specific context and discusses interest in multiple models. The design question is which context, tools and definitions should remain under company control when the model changes. Huber’s doctrine distinguishes company context and tools from rented model capability and commodity infrastructure. This is a useful procurement question: which assets would be required to reproduce the service or improve it with a different supplier? Sehgal argues that an AI agent's operational continuity resides in its durable event history—the recorded sequence of prompts, retrieved context, tool calls, and user interventions—rather than in the runtime or model executing it. Consequently, log ownership can become an important switching cost: if an organization cannot inspect, export, or replay this operational history, migrating an agent workflow can complicate reproducing its path-dependent behavior and accumulated context. (Adapted as an architectural pillar in Ismail's manuscript, S42).","url":"/stations/32-practical-provider-portability/"},{"id":"AI-33","kind":"station","number":33,"line":"F","line_name":"Operations & Scaling","line_color":"#078C9B","title":"Waiting for users to tell you something has broken","short":"Monitor the work","summary":"Monitor whether the service is doing the job correctly, not merely whether it is online.","text":"Station 33 (AI-33) Line F (Operations & Scaling): Monitor the work — Waiting for users to tell you something has broken. Monitor whether the service is doing the job correctly, not merely whether it is online. A live endpoint can return wrong, stale or unusable results. Useful observability connects task outcomes to data freshness, tool failures, costs and human review. Alerts need accountable responders and actions; otherwise telemetry is only a growing archive. Collect enough evidence to diagnose failures without turning logs into an uncontrolled copy of sensitive company information. Which failures could remain invisible while requests continue to succeed technically? What would detect stale data, silent quality loss, rising correction time or unusual spending? Who receives each alert, how will they investigate it and what are they allowed to stop? Instrument task-level outcomes and key dependencies, including freshness, tool effects, cost and review queues; distinguish technical completion from acceptance. Define service expectations and alerts tied to actionable failure conditions, with named responders and an escalation route. Use sampled expert checks and relevant regression tests where quality is not directly observable; protect and limit sensitive traces. The Diginomica note separates instructions, memory, execution environment, connections and the operating loop. A failed answer or action may originate in any of these components. This gives a more useful debugging structure than replacing the model before checking whether it received the right information and tools. The supplied YC example describes a data-query agent and the use of failed attempts to improve its future operation. The key pattern is the return path from a real task to a checked correction—not the assumption that recording usage automatically teaches the system what was right. Rao’s conceptual distinction concerns whether acting generates usable information or instead outruns feedback and creates external costs. Applied to an agent, the question is how much it can change before the organisation can observe, interpret and contain an error.","url":"/stations/33-monitor-business-behaviour/"},{"id":"AI-34","kind":"station","number":34,"line":"F","line_name":"Operations & Scaling","line_color":"#078C9B","title":"Launching without a failure and recovery plan","short":"Prepare recovery","summary":"Prepare to stop, contain, reconcile and continue the business when an AI-enabled workflow fails.","text":"Station 34 (AI-34) Line F (Operations & Scaling): Prepare recovery — Launching without a failure and recovery plan. Prepare to stop, contain, reconcile and continue the business when an AI-enabled workflow fails. Turning off an agent prevents some new actions but does not reveal what already happened or reverse external effects. A recovery plan must address credentials, partial work, affected records, users and business continuity. Define the fallback and test it before the old process or operational expertise disappears. For irreversible actions, recovery may mean remediation rather than rollback. Can authorised staff pause new work and revoke access without the original builder or supplier? How will the team identify completed, incomplete, duplicated and uncertain actions? What workable process serves the customer while the system is unavailable or untrusted? Create a runbook for pausing, inspecting, revoking, containing and communicating, with named decision-makers and preserved evidence. Define reconciliation and remediation for external effects, plus a manual or alternative-system fallback with sufficient capacity. Run a controlled recovery drill, including a supplier outage or staff absence, and verify that the restored service passes relevant tests before resuming. The supplied failure dossier includes unsafe retries, partial transactions, duplicate actions, poisoned context, weak identity boundaries and missing recovery. These are useful test categories because a superficially successful response can coexist with a damaged record or an action executed twice. The proposed architecture includes evaluations, searchable logs, constrained actions, review queues and rollback around sensing and execution. It is useful as a completeness check: a workflow is not governed merely because a person approved its original launch.","url":"/stations/34-failure-and-recovery/"},{"id":"AI-35","kind":"station","number":35,"line":"F","line_name":"Operations & Scaling","line_color":"#078C9B","title":"Treating launch as the end of ownership","short":"Own the lifecycle","summary":"Fund and assign the full service lifecycle: operation, change, support, knowledge maintenance and retirement.","text":"Station 35 (AI-35) Line F (Operations & Scaling): Own the lifecycle — Treating launch as the end of ownership. Fund and assign the full service lifecycle: operation, change, support, knowledge maintenance and retirement. A project may have a delivery team but no enduring owner after launch. Meanwhile models, data, tools, users and business rules change. Treat the deployed workflow as an operating service with accountable stewardship. Ownership should survive personnel changes and include the authority to restrict or retire a system whose value or safety no longer justifies its cost. Who owns the service after the project closes, and what resources are committed to that work? Who approves changes to the model, context, rules, access and evaluations? How will dependencies, credentials, retained data and user expectations be handled when the service is retired? Register every live workflow with operational and business owners, deputies, dependencies, permissions, costs and support routes. Fund recurring monitoring, expert review, regression testing, knowledge updates and user feedback; define change and incident responsibilities. Set reassessment and retirement triggers, then decommission deliberately: revoke access, stop schedules, preserve required evidence and communicate the replacement or closure. Shipper describes moving from personal agents toward agents responsible for team or company jobs, with continuing engineering and maintenance. This offers a concrete explanation for why an individual’s useful experiment still needs a service owner, shared context and ongoing support before others rely on it. The supplied analysis connects work traces to expert review, revised rules or tools, reuse and subsequent outcomes. It also treats a skill file as an operating asset with trigger conditions, permissions, expected output, tests and an escalation path—not merely a reusable prompt. The twelve-factor note includes evaluation rubrics, retained operational traces and encoded expertise as parts of a learning system. Together they suggest that trace collection is useful only when the organisation can interpret outcomes and revise a tested process.","url":"/stations/35-lifecycle-ownership/"},{"id":"AI-36","kind":"station","number":36,"line":"F","line_name":"Operations & Scaling","line_color":"#078C9B","title":"Assuming one success transfers unchanged across the company or portfolio","short":"Revalidate at scale","summary":"Reuse proven assets while revalidating local workflows, data, economics, authority and adoption.","text":"Station 36 (AI-36) Line F (Operations & Scaling): Revalidate at scale — Assuming one success transfers unchanged across the company or portfolio. Reuse proven assets while revalidating local workflows, data, economics, authority and adoption. A successful implementation provides a useful starting point, not a universal template. Another company may have different definitions, exceptions, system access, review capacity or customer expectations. Separate the reusable technical and learning assets from assumptions that were local to the first setting. Scale through evidence about the new context rather than through executive enthusiasm alone. Which parts of the original success depended on local data, people, incentives or system architecture? What is genuinely reusable, and what must be re-specified or tested in the new setting? Does the second organisation have the ownership and support capacity required to sustain the approach? Package reusable components with their scope, assumptions, tests, known failures and operating requirements—not only code or a success story. Run a local fit assessment and bounded validation using the receiving team’s cases, definitions, permissions and baseline. Share failures and adaptations across the portfolio, then revise the common pattern without forcing every business into identical work practices. The supplied scenarios contrast companies using similar systems but having different process clarity and decision discipline. This gives a practical reason to check local workflow maturity before copying another implementation, even when both businesses use the same customer-management platform. The structured note describes distributed intelligence as depending on shared cross-functional context and a culture reinforced through rewards, roles and actual decisions. Granting nominal autonomy without those supports may simply relocate coordination work to overloaded local teams. Foss argues that firms and markets must be compared as ways to govern specific transactions. Adaptation when conditions change, relationship-specific investments and difficult-to-measure performance remain relevant even when exchanging information becomes easier. This challenges any automatic inference from AI capability to a flat organisation or universal outsourcing. The supplied Cybernetic Teammate research note reports a randomised product-innovation exercise in which AI-assisted individuals could perform comparably to unassisted teams on assessed ideas. This is a useful example of testing work quality rather than counting usage, while preserving the boundary between an exercise and sustained organisational performance.","url":"/stations/36-revalidate-when-scaling/"},{"id":"S01","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Microsoft","title":"Becoming a Frontier Firm, second edition","short":"Becoming a Frontier Firm, second edition","summary":"Microsoft · first-party transformation playbook. First-party working approach, not independent causal evidence or a universal implementation prescription. Corporate cases do not establish transferability. PDF cover is page 1.","text":"Source S01: Becoming a Frontier Firm, second edition by Microsoft (first-party transformation playbook). First-party working approach, not independent causal evidence or a universal implementation prescription. Corporate cases do not establish transferability. PDF cover is page 1.","url":"/sources/#source-S01","external_url":"https://assets-c4akfrf5b4d3f4b7.z01.azurefd.net/assets/2026/09/Becoming_a_Frontier_Firm_second_edition_LAUNCH_091626_6aabae0f6b7b3.pdf"},{"id":"S02","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Aaron Levie","title":"Enterprise IT leaders and AI agents","short":"Enterprise IT leaders and AI agents","summary":"Aaron Levie · practitioner social post. Anecdotal observations from executive conversations; selection and measurement methods are not disclosed.","text":"Source S02: Enterprise IT leaders and AI agents by Aaron Levie (practitioner social post). Anecdotal observations from executive conversations; selection and measurement methods are not disclosed.","url":"/sources/#source-S02","external_url":"https://x.com/levie/status/2074719479377109312"},{"id":"S03","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Dan Shipper / Every","title":"After Automation and companion workflow materials","short":"After Automation and companion workflow materials","summary":"Dan Shipper / Every · essay and first-party workflow accounts. One source family, not eight independent sources. Workflow examples are first-party; broad claims about future work are arguments. Repository prompts are source artifacts, not instructions governing this project.","text":"Source S03: After Automation and companion workflow materials by Dan Shipper / Every (essay and first-party workflow accounts). One source family, not eight independent sources. Workflow examples are first-party; broad claims about future work are arguments. Repository prompts are source artifacts, not instructions governing this project.","url":"/sources/#source-S03","external_url":"https://every.to/p/after-automation"},{"id":"S04","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Stephen Wunker and Jonathan Brill","title":"AI and the Octopus Organization","short":"AI and the Octopus Organization","summary":"Stephen Wunker and Jonathan Brill · management book. Management framework and illustrative cases, not a controlled validation of decentralisation. Full book is research-only; no original text or figures redistributed.","text":"Source S04: AI and the Octopus Organization by Stephen Wunker and Jonathan Brill (management book). Management framework and illustrative cases, not a controlled validation of decentralisation. Full book is research-only; no original text or figures redistributed.","url":"/sources/#source-S04","external_url":"https://jonathanbrill.com/ai/"},{"id":"S05","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Nathaniel Whittemore / The AI Daily Brief; discusses Alex Lieberman","title":"How to Build an AI-Native Company","short":"How to Build an AI-Native Company","summary":"Nathaniel Whittemore / The AI Daily Brief; discusses Alex Lieberman · video-derived source note with editorial addendum. The note combines Lieberman, presenter commentary and a compiler reply-analysis addendum. Reply attributions and original video wording were not independently verified. These layers are distinguished in evidence records.","text":"Source S05: How to Build an AI-Native Company by Nathaniel Whittemore / The AI Daily Brief; discusses Alex Lieberman (video-derived source note with editorial addendum). The note combines Lieberman, presenter commentary and a compiler reply-analysis addendum. Reply attributions and original video wording were not independently verified. These layers are distinguished in evidence records.","url":"/sources/#source-S05","external_url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qa4juJzoOTY"},{"id":"S06","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Nicolai J. Foss","title":"AI Won’t Kill the Firm","short":"AI Won’t Kill the Firm","summary":"Nicolai J. Foss · economic argument. Theoretical reasoning about comparative governance, not measured predictions of firm size.","text":"Source S06: AI Won’t Kill the Firm by Nicolai J. Foss (economic argument). Theoretical reasoning about comparative governance, not measured predictions of firm size.","url":"/sources/#source-S06","external_url":"https://nicolaijfoss.substack.com/p/ai-wont-kill-the-firm"},{"id":"S07","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Benedict Evans","title":"Predicting AI Job Exposure","short":"Predicting AI Job Exposure","summary":"Benedict Evans · industry analysis. Supplied analytical extraction, not the original article. Historical analogies do not predict future employment; chart data not independently checked.","text":"Source S07: Predicting AI Job Exposure by Benedict Evans (industry analysis). Supplied analytical extraction, not the original article. Historical analogies do not predict future employment; chart data not independently checked.","url":"/sources/#source-S07","external_url":"https://www.ben-evans.com/benedictevans/2026/5/24/ai-job-exposure"},{"id":"S08","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Jack Dorsey and Roelof Botha / Block","title":"From Hierarchy to Intelligence","short":"From Hierarchy to Intelligence","summary":"Jack Dorsey and Roelof Botha / Block · corporate strategy proposal. Proposed organisational architecture, not evidence of completed deployment or measured improvement. Historical claims are not independently verified.","text":"Source S08: From Hierarchy to Intelligence by Jack Dorsey and Roelof Botha / Block (corporate strategy proposal). Proposed organisational architecture, not evidence of completed deployment or measured improvement. Historical claims are not independently verified.","url":"/sources/#source-S08","external_url":"https://block.xyz/inside/from-hierarchy-to-intelligence"},{"id":"S10","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Teresa Tung and Philippe Roussiere / CMR Insights","title":"Tacit Knowledge Is Your Next Competitive Moat","short":"Tacit Knowledge Is Your Next Competitive Moat","summary":"Teresa Tung and Philippe Roussiere / CMR Insights · practitioner article represented in analytical notes. Practitioner article, not a peer-reviewed trial. Anonymised cases and claimed competitive advantage are not independently validated; later analytical sections are the compiler’s.","text":"Source S10: Tacit Knowledge Is Your Next Competitive Moat by Teresa Tung and Philippe Roussiere / CMR Insights (practitioner article represented in analytical notes). Practitioner article, not a peer-reviewed trial. Anonymised cases and claimed competitive advantage are not independently validated; later analytical sections are the compiler’s.","url":"/sources/#source-S10","external_url":"https://cmr.berkeley.edu/2026/03/tacit-knowledge-is-your-next-competitive-moat/"},{"id":"S11","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Cedric Chin / Commoncog","title":"When Action Beats Prediction","short":"When Action Beats Prediction","summary":"Cedric Chin / Commoncog · practitioner synthesis of effectuation research. Underlying research not independently retrieved. Affordable-loss reasoning is a design lens, not proof that all experiments create value.","text":"Source S11: When Action Beats Prediction by Cedric Chin / Commoncog (practitioner synthesis of effectuation research). Underlying research not independently retrieved. Affordable-loss reasoning is a design lens, not proof that all experiments create value.","url":"/sources/#source-S11","external_url":"https://commoncog.com/when-action-beats-prediction/"},{"id":"S12","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Fabrizio Dell’Acqua and coauthors","title":"The Cybernetic Teammate","short":"The Cybernetic Teammate","summary":"Fabrizio Dell’Acqua and coauthors · research-paper summary. Only supplied research notes reviewed. Working-paper sample and later journal version differ; do not mix their denominators or effects. Short product-innovation exercise is not longitudinal job replacement evidence.","text":"Source S12: The Cybernetic Teammate by Fabrizio Dell’Acqua and coauthors (research-paper summary). Only supplied research notes reviewed. Working-paper sample and later journal version differ; do not mix their denominators or effects. Short product-innovation exercise is not longitudinal job replacement evidence.","url":"/sources/#source-S12","external_url":"https://www.hbs.edu/faculty/Pages/item.aspx?num=67197"},{"id":"S13","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"David Ondrej; Alex Lieberman and Dan of 10X","title":"10X Agentic Engineering Setup","short":"10X Agentic Engineering Setup","summary":"David Ondrej; Alex Lieberman and Dan of 10X · podcast transcript and derived cheatsheet. Transcript may contain automated errors. Cheatsheet is derived, not independent evidence; service catalog and implementation labels include editorial recommendations. Commercial claims unverified.","text":"Source S13: 10X Agentic Engineering Setup by David Ondrej; Alex Lieberman and Dan of 10X (podcast transcript and derived cheatsheet). Transcript may contain automated errors. Cheatsheet is derived, not independent evidence; service catalog and implementation labels include editorial recommendations. Commercial claims unverified.","url":"/sources/#source-S13","external_url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QBfXiWvM0qc"},{"id":"S14","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Ian Thomas / Diginomica; additional corpus analysis","title":"The enterprise AI agent harness matters more than the model","short":"The enterprise AI agent harness matters more than the model","summary":"Ian Thomas / Diginomica; additional corpus analysis · technical practitioner analysis. Supplied analytical note, not original article text. Risk dossiers and proposed enterprise architecture include compiler extensions, explicitly labelled where used. No universal model-versus-harness performance ranking established.","text":"Source S14: The enterprise AI agent harness matters more than the model by Ian Thomas / Diginomica; additional corpus analysis (technical practitioner analysis). Supplied analytical note, not original article text. Risk dossiers and proposed enterprise architecture include compiler extensions, explicitly labelled where used. No universal model-versus-harness performance ranking established.","url":"/sources/#source-S14","external_url":"https://diginomica.com/enterprise-ai-agent-harness-matters-more-model"},{"id":"S15","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Dwarkesh Patel with Alex Imas and Phil Trammell","title":"What Remains Scarce After AGI?","short":"What Remains Scarce After AGI?","summary":"Dwarkesh Patel with Alex Imas and Phil Trammell · economic scenario discussion. Scenario analysis, not a forecast or empirical effect estimate. Professional responsibility discussion is not legal advice.","text":"Source S15: What Remains Scarce After AGI? by Dwarkesh Patel with Alex Imas and Phil Trammell (economic scenario discussion). Scenario analysis, not a forecast or empirical effect estimate. Professional responsibility discussion is not legal advice.","url":"/sources/#source-S15","external_url":"https://www.dwarkesh.com/p/alex-imas-phil-trammell"},{"id":"S16","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Jaya Gupta and Ashu Garg / Foundation Capital; corpus analysis","title":"Context Graphs","short":"Context Graphs","summary":"Jaya Gupta and Ashu Garg / Foundation Capital; corpus analysis · venture-capital thesis and editorial extensions. Commercial thesis; governance model and risk tables include substantial compiler synthesis. A stored decision trace is not proof of correctness, authority or competitive advantage.","text":"Source S16: Context Graphs by Jaya Gupta and Ashu Garg / Foundation Capital; corpus analysis (venture-capital thesis and editorial extensions). Commercial thesis; governance model and risk tables include substantial compiler synthesis. A stored decision trace is not proof of correctness, authority or competitive advantage.","url":"/sources/#source-S16","external_url":"https://foundationcapital.com/ideas/context-graphs-ais-trillion-dollar-opportunity"},{"id":"S18","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Jim O’Shaughnessy with Sangeet Paul Choudary","title":"How AI Will Reshape the Knowledge Economy, Infinite Loops 323","short":"How AI Will Reshape the Knowledge Economy, Infinite Loops 323","summary":"Jim O’Shaughnessy with Sangeet Paul Choudary · edited podcast notes. Strategic hypotheses and analogies. Later critical-tension sections are compiler analysis. Workforce and macroeconomic claims not treated as established.","text":"Source S18: How AI Will Reshape the Knowledge Economy, Infinite Loops 323 by Jim O’Shaughnessy with Sangeet Paul Choudary (edited podcast notes). Strategic hypotheses and analogies. Later critical-tension sections are compiler analysis. Workforce and macroeconomic claims not treated as established.","url":"/sources/#source-S18","external_url":"https://www.infiniteloopspodcast.com/sangeet-choudary-how-ai-will-reshape-the-knowledge-economy-ep-323/"},{"id":"S19","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Lenny Rachitsky with Anish Acharya","title":"Why Companies Are Becoming a Series of Loops","short":"Why Companies Are Becoming a Series of Loops","summary":"Lenny Rachitsky with Anish Acharya · podcast transcript and summary. Investor/practitioner opinions. Model prices, capabilities and product-specific examples are time-sensitive and not independently verified; no current product recommendations made.","text":"Source S19: Why Companies Are Becoming a Series of Loops by Lenny Rachitsky with Anish Acharya (podcast transcript and summary). Investor/practitioner opinions. Model prices, capabilities and product-specific examples are time-sensitive and not independently verified; no current product recommendations made.","url":"/sources/#source-S19","external_url":"https://www.lennysnewsletter.com/p/why-companies-are-becoming-a-series"},{"id":"S22","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Luke Pierce / Boom Automations","title":"The Full Guide to Building an AI-Native Operating System","short":"The Full Guide to Building an AI-Native Operating System","summary":"Luke Pierce / Boom Automations · promotional practitioner guide. Link identifies companion post and three-page paper, not a confirmed canonical URL for the long guide. Case metrics and broad superiority claims unverified. Small-business consolidation advice is not a universal enterprise architecture.","text":"Source S22: The Full Guide to Building an AI-Native Operating System by Luke Pierce / Boom Automations (promotional practitioner guide). Link identifies companion post and three-page paper, not a confirmed canonical URL for the long guide. Case metrics and broad superiority claims unverified. Small-business consolidation advice is not a universal enterprise architecture.","url":"/sources/#source-S22","external_url":"https://x.com/lukepierceops/status/2089327211224400348"},{"id":"S24","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Mark Ajzenstadt","title":"No Process, No Agent","short":"No Process, No Agent","summary":"Mark Ajzenstadt · practitioner scenarios. Promotional practitioner account, not a representative study. Timelines and unsupported enterprise failure percentages excluded.","text":"Source S24: No Process, No Agent by Mark Ajzenstadt (practitioner scenarios). Promotional practitioner account, not a representative study. Timelines and unsupported enterprise failure percentages excluded.","url":"/sources/#source-S24","external_url":"https://x.com/mardehaym/article/2087086419491647589"},{"id":"S25","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Salim Ismail on Moonshots","title":"Organizational Singularity / ExO 3.0","short":"Organizational Singularity / ExO 3.0","summary":"Salim Ismail on Moonshots · edited podcast notes. Speculative architecture and transition framework. Workforce reductions and dramatic multiplier forecasts excluded. Parallel-run pattern retained as a proposal, not validated general practice.","text":"Source S25: Organizational Singularity / ExO 3.0 by Salim Ismail on Moonshots (edited podcast notes). Speculative architecture and transition framework. Workforce reductions and dramatic multiplier forecasts excluded. Parallel-run pattern retained as a proposal, not validated general practice.","url":"/sources/#source-S25","external_url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=I9c8STV7Hnw"},{"id":"S27","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Cal Newport / The New Yorker","title":"Why A.I. Didn’t Transform Our Lives in 2025","short":"Why A.I. Didn’t Transform Our Lives in 2025","summary":"Cal Newport / The New Yorker · dated technology commentary. Supplied analytical extraction; diagnosis pertains to late 2025, not current product capability. Not a systematic adoption survey.","text":"Source S27: Why A.I. Didn’t Transform Our Lives in 2025 by Cal Newport / The New Yorker (dated technology commentary). Supplied analytical extraction; diagnosis pertains to late 2025, not current product capability. Not a systematic adoption survey.","url":"/sources/#source-S27","external_url":"https://www.newyorker.com/culture/2025-in-review/why-ai-didnt-transform-our-lives-in-2025"},{"id":"S28","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Sangeet Paul Choudary","title":"Reshuffle: Who Wins When AI Restacks the Knowledge Economy","short":"Reshuffle: Who Wins When AI Restacks the Knowledge Economy","summary":"Sangeet Paul Choudary · strategy book. Strategic framework and cases, not causal proof of a universal new firm structure. Full research copy not redistributed.","text":"Source S28: Reshuffle: Who Wins When AI Restacks the Knowledge Economy by Sangeet Paul Choudary (strategy book). Strategic framework and cases, not causal proof of a universal new firm structure. Full research copy not redistributed.","url":"/sources/#source-S28","external_url":"https://reshufflebook.com/"},{"id":"S30","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Anton Shemerey / Sphere","title":"Tacit Knowledge Management and AI","short":"Tacit Knowledge Management and AI","summary":"Anton Shemerey / Sphere · vendor practitioner article represented in notes. Vendor cases and accuracy/speed metrics lack disclosed independent evaluation. Later governance interpretation is supplied editorial analysis.","text":"Source S30: Tacit Knowledge Management and AI by Anton Shemerey / Sphere (vendor practitioner article represented in notes). Vendor cases and accuracy/speed metrics lack disclosed independent evaluation. Later governance interpretation is supplied editorial analysis.","url":"/sources/#source-S30","external_url":"https://www.sphereinc.com/blogs/tacit-knowledge-management-ai"},{"id":"S31","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Taylor Pearson","title":"A Lever Made of Agents","short":"A Lever Made of Agents","summary":"Taylor Pearson · practitioner essay and editorial analysis. Conceptual/commercial thesis. Practical gates and failure dossiers partly compiler-derived; operating leverage does not establish durable competitive advantage.","text":"Source S31: A Lever Made of Agents by Taylor Pearson (practitioner essay and editorial analysis). Conceptual/commercial thesis. Practical gates and failure dossiers partly compiler-derived; operating leverage does not establish durable competitive advantage.","url":"/sources/#source-S31","external_url":"https://taylorpearson.substack.com/p/a-lever-made-of-agents"},{"id":"S32","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Taylor Pearson","title":"Why History’s Greatest Innovators Optimized for Interesting","short":"Why History’s Greatest Innovators Optimized for Interesting","summary":"Taylor Pearson · strategic essay. Curiosity is a proposed search heuristic. Historical and causal assertions not independently verified.","text":"Source S32: Why History’s Greatest Innovators Optimized for Interesting by Taylor Pearson (strategic essay). Curiosity is a proposed search heuristic. Historical and causal assertions not independently verified.","url":"/sources/#source-S32","external_url":"https://taylorpearson.me/interesting/"},{"id":"S34","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"David Brooks / The Atlantic","title":"The People Who Will Thrive in the AI Age","short":"The People Who Will Thrive in the AI Age","summary":"David Brooks / The Atlantic · opinion essay with secondary research references. Opinion and secondary research reporting. Psychological groupings and social forecasts are not validated workplace diagnoses. Numerical study claims omitted pending primary-source review.","text":"Source S34: The People Who Will Thrive in the AI Age by David Brooks / The Atlantic (opinion essay with secondary research references). Opinion and secondary research reporting. Psychological groupings and social forecasts are not validated workplace diagnoses. Numerical study claims omitted pending primary-source review.","url":"/sources/#source-S34","external_url":"https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/2026/06/ai-open-ai-anthropic/687689/"},{"id":"S35","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Tom Blomfield / YC Root Access","title":"Building and Structuring an AI-Native Company","short":"Building and Structuring an AI-Native Company","summary":"Tom Blomfield / YC Root Access · transcript-derived practitioner notes. Edited user-supplied transcript; publication date not confirmed. First-party YC examples and forecasts unverified. Critical-tension sections are compiler commentary.","text":"Source S35: Building and Structuring an AI-Native Company by Tom Blomfield / YC Root Access (transcript-derived practitioner notes). Edited user-supplied transcript; publication date not confirmed. First-party YC examples and forecasts unverified. Critical-tension sections are compiler commentary.","url":"/sources/#source-S35","external_url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z3JyAqh4ixg"},{"id":"S36","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Jeff Huber","title":"12-Factor Companies","short":"12-Factor Companies","summary":"Jeff Huber · practitioner design doctrine. Heuristics, not empirical validation. Default-open information and agent-first hiring prescriptions need contextual limits.","text":"Source S36: 12-Factor Companies by Jeff Huber (practitioner design doctrine). Heuristics, not empirical validation. Default-open information and agent-first hiring prescriptions need contextual limits.","url":"/sources/#source-S36","external_url":"https://jeffhuber.substack.com/p/12-factor-companies"},{"id":"S38","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Venkatesh Rao","title":"A Camera, Not an Engine II","short":"A Camera, Not an Engine II","summary":"Venkatesh Rao · conceptual technology essay. Speculative lens for action and feedback; not a controlled study or quantitative law.","text":"Source S38: A Camera, Not an Engine II by Venkatesh Rao (conceptual technology essay). Speculative lens for action and feedback; not a controlled study or quantitative law.","url":"/sources/#source-S38","external_url":"https://contraptions.venkateshrao.com/p/a-camera-not-an-engine-ii"},{"id":"S39","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Garry Tan / Y Combinator","title":"Own Your Intelligence / What Founders Can Learn From Spinoza","short":"Own Your Intelligence / What Founders Can Learn From Spinoza","summary":"Garry Tan / Y Combinator · edited talk transcript. Automated transcript edited by the compiler. Personal-system claims unverified. Ownership/local hosting is not accepted as a sufficient security guarantee.","text":"Source S39: Own Your Intelligence / What Founders Can Learn From Spinoza by Garry Tan / Y Combinator (edited talk transcript). Automated transcript edited by the compiler. Personal-system claims unverified. Ownership/local hosting is not accepted as a sufficient security guarantee.","url":"/sources/#source-S39","external_url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eRrc1pUY5oU"},{"id":"S40","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Caroline Chin and Alex Martin Richmond / OpenAI Economic Research","title":"Work at the Frontier","short":"Work at the Frontier","summary":"Caroline Chin and Alex Martin Richmond / OpenAI Economic Research · research-report summary. Only supplied notes reviewed. Descriptive task-use data, not causal productivity or demonstrated expertise. Individual-account message sample is not enterprise production outcomes; denominators and versions must remain distinct.","text":"Source S40: Work at the Frontier by Caroline Chin and Alex Martin Richmond / OpenAI Economic Research (research-report summary). Only supplied notes reviewed. Descriptive task-use data, not causal productivity or demonstrated expertise. Individual-account message sample is not enterprise production outcomes; denominators and versions must remain distinct.","url":"/sources/#source-S40","external_url":"https://openai.com/index/how-ai-is-expanding-what-people-do-at-work/"},{"id":"S41","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Salim Ismail","title":"Your AI Strategy Is Measuring the Wrong Things","short":"Your AI Strategy Is Measuring the Wrong Things","summary":"Salim Ismail · video presentation and transcript. Executive presentation and diagnostic heuristics. Proposed operational thresholds (such as the 50% time-shift test) and maturity ladders are conceptual frameworks rather than validated empirical laws. Human override rate is presented as an ungameable metric, but in practice remains vulnerable to reviewer fatigue, changing case complexity, and unmeasured escaped defects.","text":"Source S41: Your AI Strategy Is Measuring the Wrong Things by Salim Ismail (video presentation and transcript). Executive presentation and diagnostic heuristics. Proposed operational thresholds (such as the 50% time-shift test) and maturity ladders are conceptual frameworks rather than validated empirical laws. Human override rate is presented as an ungameable metric, but in practice remains vulnerable to reviewer fatigue, changing case complexity, and unmeasured escaped defects.","url":"/sources/#source-S41","external_url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Hvr8t-w9GCE"},{"id":"S42","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Salim Ismail with contributors","title":"The Organizational Singularity: How AI Breaks the Firm and Rewrites It to Solve Everything","short":"The Organizational Singularity: How AI Breaks the Firm and Rewrites It to Solve Everything","summary":"Salim Ismail with contributors · book manuscript (v25). Prescriptive organizational transformation manuscript and directional forecasts. 2036 scenarios, radical workforce reduction ratios, and specific proprietary frameworks (ExO 3.0, Intelligence Stack, REWRITE) are forward-looking hypotheses rather than established empirical findings. Steelman counterarguments (such as the hidden costs of delayering and the missing junior apprenticeship loop) are acknowledged within the text.","text":"Source S42: The Organizational Singularity: How AI Breaks the Firm and Rewrites It to Solve Everything by Salim Ismail with contributors (book manuscript (v25)). Prescriptive organizational transformation manuscript and directional forecasts. 2036 scenarios, radical workforce reduction ratios, and specific proprietary frameworks (ExO 3.0, Intelligence Stack, REWRITE) are forward-looking hypotheses rather than established empirical findings. Steelman counterarguments (such as the hidden costs of delayering and the missing junior apprenticeship loop) are acknowledged within the text.","url":"/sources/#source-S42","external_url":"https://www.organizationalsingularity.com/"},{"id":"S43","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Salim Ismail","title":"What Happens When Intelligence Becomes Almost Free","short":"What Happens When Intelligence Becomes Almost Free","summary":"Salim Ismail · video presentation and breakdown. Conceptual breakdown of low-cost contextual micro-decisions versus broad autonomous agents. Vendor marketing claims regarding 'zero hallucinations' are unverified and contradicted by the probabilistic nature of neural models. The 74% labor claim in the video commentary conflates a specific APQC workflow monotonicity result with aggregate enterprise employment.","text":"Source S43: What Happens When Intelligence Becomes Almost Free by Salim Ismail (video presentation and breakdown). Conceptual breakdown of low-cost contextual micro-decisions versus broad autonomous agents. Vendor marketing claims regarding 'zero hallucinations' are unverified and contradicted by the probabilistic nature of neural models. The 74% labor claim in the video commentary conflates a specific APQC workflow monotonicity result with aggregate enterprise employment.","url":"/sources/#source-S43","external_url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R4AnpMknO4o"},{"id":"S44","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Harang Ju","title":"When Coordination Is Avoidable: A Monotonicity Analysis of Organizational Tasks","short":"When Coordination Is Avoidable: A Monotonicity Analysis of Organizational Tasks","summary":"Harang Ju · academic preprint (arXiv:2602.18673). Preprint from Johns Hopkins Carey Business School, not yet peer-reviewed. Applies the distributed-systems CALM theorem to Thompson's task interdependence taxonomy. Evaluates 65 APQC enterprise workflows (finding 74% monotonic) and 13,417 O*NET occupational tasks (finding 42% monotonic). The author explicitly notes that monotonicity guarantees only correctness under asynchronous execution, not output quality or organizational coherence.","text":"Source S44: When Coordination Is Avoidable: A Monotonicity Analysis of Organizational Tasks by Harang Ju (academic preprint (arXiv:2602.18673)). Preprint from Johns Hopkins Carey Business School, not yet peer-reviewed. Applies the distributed-systems CALM theorem to Thompson's task interdependence taxonomy. Evaluates 65 APQC enterprise workflows (finding 74% monotonic) and 13,417 O*NET occupational tasks (finding 42% monotonic). The author explicitly notes that monotonicity guarantees only correctness under asynchronous execution, not output quality or organizational coherence.","url":"/sources/#source-S44","external_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18673"},{"id":"S45","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Ishaan Sehgal (Omnara)","title":"The Log Is the Agent","short":"The Log Is the Agent","summary":"Ishaan Sehgal (Omnara) · practitioner technical essay and keynote. Vendor-origin architectural argument and position paper by the founder of Omnara (an open-source agent command center), also delivered as an AI Engineer World's Fair keynote. Proposes treating append-only event logs as the persistent definition of an agent rather than models or runtime infrastructure. Retaining, redacting, and replaying extensive agent telemetry creates substantial data-governance, storage, and privacy overhead in enterprise settings.","text":"Source S45: The Log Is the Agent by Ishaan Sehgal (Omnara) (practitioner technical essay and keynote). Vendor-origin architectural argument and position paper by the founder of Omnara (an open-source agent command center), also delivered as an AI Engineer World's Fair keynote. Proposes treating append-only event logs as the persistent definition of an agent rather than models or runtime infrastructure. Retaining, redacting, and replaying extensive agent telemetry creates substantial data-governance, storage, and privacy overhead in enterprise settings.","url":"/sources/#source-S45","external_url":"https://www.omnara.com/blog/the-log-is-the-agent"},{"id":"S46","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Bartek Pucek (@barticz)","title":"Thinking Companies","short":"Thinking Companies","summary":"Bartek Pucek (@barticz) · practitioner essay and commercial thesis. Practitioner essay and commercial positioning statement by the founder of The Thinking Company and Runstate. Argues that AI enables continuous operational re-examination of stale process assumptions and unbundles the job bundle, while synthesizing the AI-native services model (Julien Bek, Bérenger Teboul-Danguin, Jake Saper). The supply-chain case is an illustrative scenario rather than an independently verified enterprise case study; claims of reaching Big Four consulting scale with minimal headcount remain unverified venture forecasts.","text":"Source S46: Thinking Companies by Bartek Pucek (@barticz) (practitioner essay and commercial thesis). Practitioner essay and commercial positioning statement by the founder of The Thinking Company and Runstate. Argues that AI enables continuous operational re-examination of stale process assumptions and unbundles the job bundle, while synthesizing the AI-native services model (Julien Bek, Bérenger Teboul-Danguin, Jake Saper). The supply-chain case is an illustrative scenario rather than an independently verified enterprise case study; claims of reaching Big Four consulting scale with minimal headcount remain unverified venture forecasts.","url":"/sources/#source-S46","external_url":"https://x.com/barticz/status/2108184398894145626"},{"id":"S47","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Julien Bek (Sequoia Capital)","title":"Services: The New Software","short":"Services: The New Software","summary":"Julien Bek (Sequoia Capital) · venture capital thesis and market analysis. Venture capital investment essay framing the expansion from SaaS software budgets to services/labor budgets. Synthesizes the copilot vs. autopilot distinction, gross margin evolution from services (30–40%) to software (70–80%), and the difference between commoditized intelligence and defensible judgment. Market size expansion models and the ease of transitioning agency services into software-margin businesses remain speculative venture hypotheses requiring empirical validation.","text":"Source S47: Services: The New Software by Julien Bek (Sequoia Capital) (venture capital thesis and market analysis). Venture capital investment essay framing the expansion from SaaS software budgets to services/labor budgets. Synthesizes the copilot vs. autopilot distinction, gross margin evolution from services (30–40%) to software (70–80%), and the difference between commoditized intelligence and defensible judgment. Market size expansion models and the ease of transitioning agency services into software-margin businesses remain speculative venture hypotheses requiring empirical validation.","url":"/sources/#source-S47","external_url":"https://sequoiacap.com/article/services-the-new-software"},{"id":"S48","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Jake Saper, Lotti Siniscalco, Rishub Nahar, Kabir Sial (Emergence Capital)","title":"The AI-Native Services Playbook","short":"The AI-Native Services Playbook","summary":"Jake Saper, Lotti Siniscalco, Rishub Nahar, Kabir Sial (Emergence Capital) · practitioner operating handbook and venture thesis. Venture capital operating handbook based on early-stage portfolio benchmarks. Provides tactical operating metrics for AI-native services including Mirage PMF, honest COGS accounting (treating inference and human review as COGS rather than OPEX), HURT (Human Unit Review Time), and paired engineering. Performance benchmarks, gross margin convergence timelines, and long-term moat defensibility reflect early-stage company observations and require independent enterprise verification.","text":"Source S48: The AI-Native Services Playbook by Jake Saper, Lotti Siniscalco, Rishub Nahar, Kabir Sial (Emergence Capital) (practitioner operating handbook and venture thesis). Venture capital operating handbook based on early-stage portfolio benchmarks. Provides tactical operating metrics for AI-native services including Mirage PMF, honest COGS accounting (treating inference and human review as COGS rather than OPEX), HURT (Human Unit Review Time), and paired engineering. Performance benchmarks, gross margin convergence timelines, and long-term moat defensibility reflect early-stage company observations and require independent enterprise verification.","url":"/sources/#source-S48","external_url":"https://www.emcap.com/thoughts/the-ai-native-services-playbook"},{"id":"S49","kind":"source","number":null,"line":null,"author":"Konstantine Buhler (Sequoia Capital)","title":"The Cognitive Revolution (When Machines Do the Thinking)","short":"The Cognitive Revolution (When Machines Do the Thinking)","summary":"Konstantine Buhler (Sequoia Capital) · macroeconomic and philosophical essay. Broad historical and macroeconomic essay comparing the transition from physical automation (the Industrial Revolution) to cognitive automation (the Cognitive Revolution). Formulates the Jevons paradox of cognition ('Most problems on Earth currently go un-thought-about, because thinking is expensive'). It provides a conceptual framing of expanding cognitive demand rather than an empirical or operational implementation blueprint.","text":"Source S49: The Cognitive Revolution (When Machines Do the Thinking) by Konstantine Buhler (Sequoia Capital) (macroeconomic and philosophical essay). Broad historical and macroeconomic essay comparing the transition from physical automation (the Industrial Revolution) to cognitive automation (the Cognitive Revolution). Formulates the Jevons paradox of cognition ('Most problems on Earth currently go un-thought-about, because thinking is expensive'). It provides a conceptual framing of expanding cognitive demand rather than an empirical or operational implementation blueprint.","url":"/sources/#source-S49","external_url":"https://sequoiacap.com/article/the-cognitive-revolution"}],"previews":[{"id":"AI-01","number":1,"line":"A","title":"Strategy, not licences","summary":"Define the business change before treating tool access as progress.","url":"/stations/01-licences-are-not-strategy/"},{"id":"AI-02","number":2,"line":"A","title":"Understand the work","summary":"Map real work, including its exceptions and informal decisions, before deciding what to automate.","url":"/stations/02-understand-before-automating/"},{"id":"AI-03","number":3,"line":"A","title":"Value over spectacle","summary":"Choose useful problems and make room for bounded discovery—not just impressive demonstrations.","url":"/stations/03-value-before-spectacle/"},{"id":"AI-04","number":4,"line":"A","title":"Use simpler software","summary":"Use AI where interpretation helps and ordinary software where explicit rules are sufficient.","url":"/stations/04-simplest-effective-system/"},{"id":"AI-05","number":5,"line":"A","title":"Measure outcomes","summary":"Connect usage to accepted work and accepted work to an actual business outcome.","url":"/stations/05-measure-business-improvement/"},{"id":"AI-06","number":6,"line":"A","title":"Prove the economics","summary":"Establish capability inside a budget, then optimise the complete cost of successful delivery.","url":"/stations/06-economics-after-feasibility/"},{"id":"AI-07","number":7,"line":"B","title":"Business ownership","summary":"Join executive sponsorship, process ownership and technical delivery without pretending one role can do all three.","url":"/stations/07-business-ownership/"},{"id":"AI-08","number":8,"line":"B","title":"Avoid the bottleneck","summary":"Centralise reusable support and essential controls while keeping local outcomes and workflow knowledge close to the work.","url":"/stations/08-central-team-without-bottleneck/"},{"id":"AI-09","number":9,"line":"B","title":"Learn in real work","summary":"Develop role-specific capability through real tasks, feedback and protected practice.","url":"/stations/09-learning-in-real-work/"},{"id":"AI-10","number":10,"line":"B","title":"Govern the prototype","summary":"Give non-technical builders a supported route from useful experiment to owned, tested service.","url":"/stations/10-prototype-to-production/"},{"id":"AI-11","number":11,"line":"B","title":"Bring risk partners in","summary":"Design a workable route for responsible experimentation with the people who own its constraints.","url":"/stations/11-risk-partners-from-start/"},{"id":"AI-12","number":12,"line":"B","title":"Rebuild trust","summary":"Address trust, incentives, workload and role uncertainty rather than assume another course will solve adoption.","url":"/stations/12-resistance-incentives-trust/"},{"id":"AI-13","number":13,"line":"B","title":"Keep your judgement","summary":"Keep the capacity to judge the work, challenge its assumptions and accept responsibility for its consequences.","url":"/stations/13-retain-judgement/"},{"id":"AI-14","number":14,"line":"C","title":"Fix useful data","summary":"Prepare the data needed for the chosen use case without making every project depend on a complete enterprise rebuild.","url":"/stations/14-use-case-data-foundation/"},{"id":"AI-15","number":15,"line":"C","title":"Define the terms","summary":"Make business meaning, calculation rules and source authority explicit for each question.","url":"/stations/15-shared-business-definitions/"},{"id":"AI-16","number":16,"line":"C","title":"Know the blind spots","summary":"Make the boundary of the evidence visible: coverage, missingness, history and freshness.","url":"/stations/16-data-coverage-and-limits/"},{"id":"AI-17","number":17,"line":"C","title":"Capture the know-how","summary":"Capture decision-relevant expertise while preserving the people and practice that keep it current.","url":"/stations/17-capture-and-renew-expertise/"},{"id":"AI-18","number":18,"line":"C","title":"Retrieve what matters","summary":"Provide permission-aware retrieval that returns the right evidence and makes retrieval failures diagnosable.","url":"/stations/18-relevant-retrieval/"},{"id":"AI-19","number":19,"line":"C","title":"Test integrations","summary":"Test business semantics and partial effects, not just successful API connections.","url":"/stations/19-integration-behaviour/"},{"id":"AI-20","number":20,"line":"D","title":"Enforce permissions","summary":"Enforce what an agent may read and do in software, using identities and permissions appropriate to its job.","url":"/stations/20-enforced-agent-permissions/"},{"id":"AI-21","number":21,"line":"D","title":"Protect data by design","summary":"Make approved data handling understandable and supported by technical controls across the information lifecycle.","url":"/stations/21-data-protection-by-design/"},{"id":"AI-22","number":22,"line":"D","title":"Distrust outside instructions","summary":"Treat retrieved documents, messages and tool results as evidence to inspect—not as authority to change the agent’s rules.","url":"/stations/22-untrusted-instructions/"},{"id":"AI-23","number":23,"line":"D","title":"Set hard limits","summary":"Bound the amount of work, money and external effect an agent can authorise before the system stops or seeks approval.","url":"/stations/23-bounded-agent-execution/"},{"id":"AI-24","number":24,"line":"D","title":"Match controls to risk","summary":"Calibrate autonomy and assurance to each decision’s consequences, uncertainty and reversibility.","url":"/stations/24-controls-proportionate-to-risk/"},{"id":"AI-25","number":25,"line":"E","title":"Define good results","summary":"Turn business expectations into representative cases, acceptance criteria and boundaries for abstention.","url":"/stations/25-define-good-results/"},{"id":"AI-26","number":26,"line":"E","title":"Test the hard cases","summary":"Use domain users, exceptions and adversarial cases to test the boundaries of the entire workflow.","url":"/stations/26-test-difficult-and-adversarial-cases/"},{"id":"AI-27","number":27,"line":"E","title":"Debug the whole system","summary":"Diagnose model, context, retrieval, tool and orchestration failures separately, then evaluate the complete configuration.","url":"/stations/27-debug-the-whole-system/"},{"id":"AI-28","number":28,"line":"E","title":"Plan review capacity","summary":"Plan the capacity to review, accept and use output—not just the capacity to generate it.","url":"/stations/28-review-capacity-and-throughput/"},{"id":"AI-29","number":29,"line":"E","title":"Go beyond the demo","summary":"Estimate remaining uncertainty and operational work rather than the fraction of visible features built.","url":"/stations/29-production-readiness-not-feature-count/"},{"id":"AI-30","number":30,"line":"E","title":"Decide on pilots","summary":"Give each experiment a decision and keep the programme able to act on what it learns.","url":"/stations/30-pilots-with-decisions/"},{"id":"AI-31","number":31,"line":"F","title":"Plan the handover","summary":"Buy a defined outcome and the ability to operate it, not indefinite dependence on the original supplier.","url":"/stations/31-supplier-deliverables-and-handover/"},{"id":"AI-32","number":32,"line":"F","title":"Keep switching feasible","summary":"Understand where dependence lives and test a realistic exit path without overengineering portability.","url":"/stations/32-practical-provider-portability/"},{"id":"AI-33","number":33,"line":"F","title":"Monitor the work","summary":"Monitor whether the service is doing the job correctly, not merely whether it is online.","url":"/stations/33-monitor-business-behaviour/"},{"id":"AI-34","number":34,"line":"F","title":"Prepare recovery","summary":"Prepare to stop, contain, reconcile and continue the business when an AI-enabled workflow fails.","url":"/stations/34-failure-and-recovery/"},{"id":"AI-35","number":35,"line":"F","title":"Own the lifecycle","summary":"Fund and assign the full service lifecycle: operation, change, support, knowledge maintenance and retirement.","url":"/stations/35-lifecycle-ownership/"},{"id":"AI-36","number":36,"line":"F","title":"Revalidate at scale","summary":"Reuse proven assets while revalidating local workflows, data, economics, authority and adoption.","url":"/stations/36-revalidate-when-scaling/"}]};